      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>. .. , , _ WOODS' GREAT ~ . _. „..........,,,;. ..„,,,,,,.„. ...,, Bottles 11i and 29 . . 44. Of Chemists Everywhere</head>
        <p>. .. , , _ WOODS' GREAT ~ . _. „..........,,,;. ..„,,,,,,.„. ...,, Bottles 11i and 29 . . 44. Of Chemists Everywhere</p>
        <p>the case, the three alleged assaults were of enormous importance, both as to the question of malice and in the assessment of damages. It was certainly a most extraordinary story. It was very difficult to understand why the girl did not complain immediately, and difficult also to explain what could be her motive in making false r:ccusations, if her story were untrue. It was an improbable story. There were the patients who might have heard her if she cried out, there was the nurse in the upper floor within call, anybody might have observed what happened from the corridor above, the night charge nurse might have come round at any moment, and on the second or third night there was the probability that the plaintiff would have bad somebody to protect her or to watch the defendant's behaviour. There were great improbabilities on both sides, and probably the jury would be able to find some guidance in the demeanour of the two parties in the witness-box as to which was more worthy of belief. QUESTIONS FOR THE JURY. - - - - - - _</p>
        <p>His Lordship framed the following questions for the jury :- 1. Did the defendant speak the words alleged to Mr. Awbery (the master), to Miss Biggin, and to Mr. Todd, and to the committee, on the 16th of August? 2. Did be make the statements or any or which of them, bona fide and honestly believing them to be true?</p>
        <p>3. Was he actuated by some improper or indirect motive in making such statements, or any of them, or which of them?</p>
        <p>His Lordship asked the jury to assess the damages in respect of each of the separate slanders.</p>
        <p>£225 DAMAGES.</p>
        <p>The jury, after deliberating little more than half an hour, returned the following findings :- 1. As to Mr. Awbery, yes; as to Miss Biggin, yes ; as to Mr. Todd, yes ; as to the committee, doubtful. 2. No.</p>
        <p>3. Yes ; in all of them. Damages were assessed at £75 in each of the three cases which were found proven, making £225 in all. Judgment for the plaintiff was entered accordingly, and in respect of the alleged slander to the committee judgment was entered for the defendant, his Lordship remarking that defendant was entitled to whatever costs there were on that issue.</p>
        <p>--.-..41:70...- -</p>
        <p>Agonies of Sciatica. One of Liverpool's Best Known Men describes how his torture was ended by Mr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>Mr. William Longridge is a retired police-sergeant who now holds a position of trust in one of the prin- cipal banks in Liverpool. He is 55 years of age and was for many years connected with the Liverpool Police Force, from which service he has been Pensioned off. He received special awards from the Liverpool Ship- wreck and Humane Society, and is a man highly respected throughout the city. Recently he was attacked by Sciatica in such an acute form .that he was once confined to bed for five weeks and three days. He was completely cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and he says :—" I have not felt any trace of Sciatica since."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>SIMPLE CAUSE.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE CAUSE.</p>
        <p>"What was the cause of your complaint ? "he was asked by a reporter who called upon him at his house, 354, Scotland-road, Liverpool. _ . _</p>
        <p>"Simply through getfing my feet wet," he replied. "I first felt a shivering feeling coming over me, and this turned to slight symptoms of Rheumatism. Then I began to feel as if I was paralysed in the lower extremities, and was unable to move without pains like hot needles running down my thighs and legs ; these pains of Sciatica nearly drove me mad. When I attempted to walk I became so faint that I had to turn back home. My left leg became</p>
        <p>. William Longridge ured of Acute Sciatica by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>almost useless, and at times swift pains shot right down to my feet. For hours I would roll and toss in bed through sheer torture, and beads of perspiration rolled off my forehead. Doctors prescribed all sorts of medicines, but I seemed to get worse. I then under- went a course of massage, but the Sciatic pains increased in fury. "My sister came to visit me and insisted that I should try Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. My wife purchased some, and I took the doses regularly. I derived a little benefit and so continued the medicine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ONLY REFECTIVE MEDICINE.</head>
        <p>THE ONLY REFECTIVE MEDICINE.</p>
        <p>"Soon I made such improvement that I felt sure Dr. Williams' Pink Pills were the only medicine to put me on my feet. In a few weeks I was able •to get up. I could eat and digest my food, and then began to take exercise without feeling any after distress. My cure took place some months ago, and since then I have not felt any trace of Sciatica's pains. "I was cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and I feel it my duty to make known the wonderful curative powers of this medicine. I found that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills restored my nervous system and nourished and enriched my blood./' Victims of Sciatica need their veins filled with the New, Pure, Rich Blood supplied by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, which have cured many of Sciatica, Rheumatism, Paralysis, St. Vitus's Dance, Gout, Neuralgia and Headache. Only the genuine pills cure and substitutes should be refused because they are worse than useless. The pills that have cured are Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People (seven words). They can be obtained of all dealers, or direct from the Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, post free, 2s. 9d. for one box or 13s. 9d. for six boxes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>CHRISTMAS MUSIC FOR THE TALKING MACHINE.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS MUSIC FOR THE TALKING MACHINE.</p>
        <p>Music and Christmas have always been associated,and the many music lovers who are owners of talking machines will be intending to make good use of their instruments this festive season. Every talking machinist will, therefore, be particularly interested in a charming little publication entitled "Christmas Music" which the Columbia Phonograph Company of 64 and 66, Oxford-street, London, W., are offering to send free to all inquirers. This interesting production contains an introductory "Christmas talk " followed by a series of "Christmas Programmes" for bcth cycliniler and disc record users—delightful compilations embracing just such hymns, songs, sketches, stories and instrumental music as everyone wants to hear at Christmas time. Apart from the very real value of the contents, the publication is well worth rending for, for the sake of the artistic cover which bears an exceedingly entertaining picture of R humorous uhristmas incident. Columbia Records fit all makes of talking machines both cylinder and disc. All owners of such instruments should, therefore, write up to the Columbia Company at the address given above for this special Christmas publication, stating whether they are interested in disc or cylinder records. They will find that it will enable them to enjoy a truly merry Christmas.</p>
        <p>BEST &amp; GOES FARTHEST.</p>
      </div>
