      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>WORKHOUSE SCANDAL.,</head>
        <p>THE WORKHOUSE SCANDAL.,</p>
        <p>Heavy Damages Against the Doctor.</p>
        <p>The action in which Edith Dinnis, probationer nurse at the Warrington Workhouse Infirmary, sued Dr. Thomas E. Flitcroft, resident medical officer of the same institution' for damages for slander was concluded at the Liverpool Assizes on Saturday before Mr. Justice Pickford and a special jury. Mr. Rigby Swift, counsel for the plaintiff, resumed his cross-examination of the defendont. In reply to Mr. Swift, Dr. Flitcroft said that he was- thirty-two yeais of age, had been a qualified practitioner for two years, had held an appointment at Newcastle, and prior to that had been a chemist. His salary for his present appointment was £l3O. He was fighting the action with his own money, and with money which bad been lent him by his father, who was now dead. He had not borrowed any money, but his brother-in-law had guaranteed his costs in writing. The Workhouse Master's evidence was right to a certain extent, but not quite, Was there anything in the world to make you think this girl, the plaintiff, was in a certain condition ? Except the rumour I had heard. From whom had you heard a rumour ?—From Nurse Gorse. You made no inquiry into the rumour at all before communicating it to the master ?—I did not. I told him of it because I thought it was my duty From the beginning to the end did you eve tell it to the committee ?—I did not; I had no opportunity. The defendant added that he did not think Nurse Dinnis was a bad girl "morally," but he knew she was lying. Mr. Swift: Did you ask Nurse Gorse from whom she had heard the rumour ?—I did ask her, and she said she had heard it from one of the patients. Did you give the master the name of the patient ?—I don't know it myself. Didn't you try to ascertain it and try to follow it up? —No. I didn't believe the rumour, and I don't even BOW. Mr. Swift: Thanks. Then, I understand you to say that you went and told the master a rumour which you didn't believe ?—Yea. and I told him I didn't believe it. Regarding three other nurses who bad left the hospital during his time, defendant said he had never heard that any of them bad left on his account until the rumour was mentioned to the committee by Miss Broadbent. One of the nurses, in fact, had written to him for a testimonial, but he had not given her one. In re-examination by Mr. Taylor, the defendant characterised as an" abominable lie" the suggestion that these three nurses bad left because of him. Annie Crowe, who had been engaged at the infirmary as temporary nurse, and was night charge nurse during July, produced her report of her nightly rounds. The plaintiff bad made no complaint to her on any of these visits, and there was nothing in her demanour to suggest anything unusual had happened. On the morning of the 25th of July, the day before Nurse Dinnis' complaint was brought before the committee, the plaintiff was very cheerful, and was playing comic songs in the sitting-room. During the time she was at the hospital the doctor had always been well-behaved towards the nurses. Walter C. Brooks, one of the Warrington Guardians, questioned as to the meeting of August 16, when the -defendant was before the Committee, said that the doctor, in answer to an inquiry as to what he had done to clear himself, read a short statement„ and added nothing further. The Rev: T. R. Matthews, of Earlestown, a member of the Warrington Board,said he could not recollect the defendant having told the Board that the plaintiff was in a pregnant condition. Mr. Swift: Do you remember a resolution being proposed that Nurse Dinnis should be summarily dis- missed, and the doctor absolved from all blame ?—Yes. Who proposed it ?—I did, if I remember rightly. William Furness, another of the Guardians, also said the doctor had made no charge against the nurse in Committee. Mr. Furness was asked in cross-examination by Mr. Swift whether it was true be had proposed a resolution on the 25th August that Nurse Dinnis should be dis- missed ?—The Witness : Yes, that is quite correct ; I proposed that. What did you propose that she should be dismissed for ?—Well I thought that as a Guardian I should not be doing my duty by the house it I allowed a girl to remain as a nurse in that institution who, on her own confession, had allowed herself to be tampered with three times without making any complaint. Mr. Justice Pickford : Allowed herself to be tampered with ; that was rather strong, wasn't it, until you had heard her story? Mr. Swift: Did the Clerk speak against that resolu- tion and say for lack of evidence it would be better not ' to pass such a resolution ?—I believe lab did. It is true that you declined to pass inch a resolution? —Yes, absolutely. My opinion did not coincide with the Clerk's at all. The resolution, proceeded the witness, was lost only by the vote of the Chairman. On the 18th the witness proposed another resolution, that Nurse Dinnis should resign, and this resolution was carried by seven votes to four, the subject, of this resolution being the complaint by the doctor about the dirty conditions of certain patients. Father Ryan, a Guardian, also deposed that he had no recollection of the defendant making the charge against the plaintiff in committee. COUNSEL'S ADDRESSES. Mr. Taylor said the first thing they looked for where a woman made a charge against a man was—did she cry out? did she complain ? And a more extraordinary case than the present one in that respect there,never had been in a court of justice. Counsel ventured to say that there never was a more extraordinary account given by a woman of three aggravated assaults—be did not say allowed, but submitted to in the sense that she never chose to make any complaint about them at the time. "Are twelve special jurymen of Liverpool," asked Mr. Taylor, " going to say, so far as it is an element in the case, that these assaults took place? Have sympathy with her if you like, but I submit it cannot be imagined that a tribunal dealing with these facts and that sort of evidence can say, We are satis- fied that these assaults took place,' when a woman tells you that extraordinary story which she has told you. It is a story which I suggest to you reasonable men cannot accept." Mr. Swift (for Nurse Dinnis) invited the jury to consider the case from the point of view of a man who forgave but never forgot; of a man who had a grievance against nearly everybody in the institution; of a man of whom the plaintiff would be afraid that his word would be taken before hers, that he might do her an injury, and that it would be better for her (as she bad stated in the box) to leave the institution altogether rather than make any complaint or create any scandal. What did the jury think of a case which had to he met by the defence by a charge against everybody who had been called on behalf of the plaintiff of some improper and unjust desire to injure the doctor? It was a foul and cruel slander, for which there had been no sugges- tion of fact. The defendant's statemant was a lie, uttered with the unscrupulous motive of blackening the girl's character for his own defence. He appealed to the jury to give the plaintiff damages substantial enough to teach the defendant that the daughters of working men forced to work under professional men had at least got the protection of their fellow-citizens. JUDGE'S SUMMING UP. His Lordship, in summing up, described the condi- tions under which the defamation of character by spoken words were actionable, without proof of special damage being obligatory. Since 1891, by virtue of an Act which bore the name of the last Speaker, slander which imputed uuchastity to a woman, was actionable without special damage. Up to that time—with some archaic exceptions which were not worth considering— it was not possible in England—although it was in Scotland—for a woman to maintain an action for spoken words imputing uncbastity unless she could prove special damage. Before briefly reviewing the evidence his Lordship remarked that it was quite clear the guardians were divided into two parties on this subject, and they were not to be complimented on the judicial spirit with which they had managed the ts hole thing. Although it was not strictly in issue in</p>
