        <p>... .., - •• . . .. .. - . . . •• • • _ - -_,. ._.• . •-, • ' ..-.*;-:..-r- ''. -,...,; • ..„, .... ~. FOR COUGHS ......,,,.,. „.......,.,..,., ..... _ ~...,_ .•., . ........., .. .....;..: •. . •.. „.,.......„..,,,. _ ..:..,.,....„ , ._.• _ , ~..:_......• ._ ... ~.,.., . ,• ...5.......,,„..._•..•• •_.:::....,,...,...5,t,....„..1.:„. :„•:.,_____ „...;••_.......,.. .... .11....,...,..•:•••.....:.,....,..„........:.. CURE ~...,.;,..„,...,,v,;..,„.4.:,.,.,,,..,.......%::..,:,,.:;.‘.::::•,..,_.,... „..,...._. _.::: ..„.............,..,...........4._....„.4..„...,,..... ...........,.. , , .....„......•",,,........• ~..., .„.., ~.. .• ...... ..._.,...,.. , .., ~•• ~...„ . ..... ..:..... ~...,...,,....•. .._..._ .. ~....... :,......, ..,..... .„ • „ .•.. • , _ .... ..••• . .•.. .... •.• ...„. „.... • , .• 4 and COLDS ~ , .. .. ... .....• . ..... .....,_,....:,. ~ ._.....,...,5. .. _ ._ .. ... •. ~.,.... . _ WOMAN'S REALM. DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>design. These collars are edged with a narrow Early-Victorian silk fringe, and have their cor-</p>
        <p>Iners weighted by heavy 'Silk tassels. In the open- ing between the crossover fronts comes a dainty vest of tucked ivory net, edged at the top by a narrow binding of the cocoa satin. Above this ' vest comes a little inner chemisette of fine Irish crochet in the same tone of ivory. The skirt is perfectly plain, very long, and cut with a slight train at the back. The waistband is made of the folded cocoa satin, and has two long pendent i ends, fringed and tasselled to match the collar, which are pulled through a dull gold buckle on ! the left side.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>-41"itre will settle about that when 1 retUrtr, said my chief quietly; and he bowed the two ladies out of the room.</p>
        <p>Steyne kept a portmanteau ready packed for travelling. In less than ten minutes he had found another, and had thrown into it a supply of neces- saries for me. In another quarter of an hour we were in the train, and slowly beginning our long northward journey. "I brought you with me for company, you know, Farrer," said Steyne, laying his hand affectionately on my knee; "but you must keep your ears and eyes open, and favour me with any impressions that suffeest themselves to you as we ao alone." am afraid, said I, that I have only one impression at present, and that is not exactly favourable to your client." "You think Mr. Macleod. poisoned his wife, and than hid her body to prevent the Post-mortem taking place? " I nodded.</p>
        <p>"The facts point to that conclusion, certainly," answered Steyne, "but if I had felt sure that that inference was the right one I should now be in my own comfortable dining-room, and not preparing to spend the night in a railway carriage. In other words, I should not have accepted this Highland Chief as a client. Wait till we see him. I ought to be able to tell you then whether he is a man' capable of poisoning his wife or not." . We reached Inverness in the eveninc, of the following day; and Mr. Munro, the Procurator Fiscal a functionary nearly equivalent to the French "Examining Magistrate" having been informed of our arrival by telegram, very civilly called upon Steyne at the Royal Hotel. He turned out to be a fussy little man, with a vast idea of the dignity of his office, which had been outraged by the rifling of Mrs. Macleod's resting-place. He spoke out very freely about the case of "Inohgoil " —so Mr. Macleod was called in the Highlands— and impressed on my employer the necessity of com- pelling the obdurate chieftain to submit himself to the demands of the law. _ __ _</p>
        <p>"I don't accuse Inchgoil of having murdered his wife," he said, in conclusion. "God forbid that I should bring such a charge against any man with- out evidence! But I do charge him with abstracting her body on purpose to delay and frustrate justice. Hoots, man! Don't tell me! If you think oily earls in Harris would dare to set his, .foot in Kil- berm much less rob a grave in it, without the Chief's permission, all I can say is, you don't ken the West Highlands of Scotland. And to rob the private vault of the Macleods! The thing's clean impossible. The Chief must have done it, actor, or art and part. Now, you must see that this brings suspicion to his door; and the longer the body is concealed, the keener the suspicion will become. If the body is not found at once the thing will get into the papers, and the Chief's good name will be ruined for ever, be he guilty or innocent of the main offence. That would be a peety, for his mother's sake—a grand old lady. You'll see her at Inchgoil. Do what you can to bring the Chief to reason, Mr. Steyne, and tell me if ye succeed. I'll wait a wee for a line from you: No more toddy, thank ye. Good-night, and a safe journey." We left Inverness early next morning, and two days later were transhipped from the steamer into a small boat, manned by five sturdy Highlanders. The boat, which had been sent to the steamer to meet us, was rowed swiftly towards a small island that lay about a mile off the coast of the island of Harris.</p>
        <p>It was dusk, and as we approached the island I saw that part, of what I had taken for rock was the Castle itself. Lights appeared here and there in the windows; and we were presently landed in a sheltered cove that lay directly under the walls. The base of the tower formed, indeed, the upper part of one of the seaward-gazing cliffs. I ivondered at the temerity of the builder, in thus exposing his work to the fury of wind and waves, but I ceased to wonder when I came to inspect the walls, and found that they were of granite and seven feet thick. Viewed as a dwelling-house, the Castle was not large. The lower storey of tha tower formed the hall, into which several rooms opened. it was adorned with curious old weapons and some magnifi- cent antlers, and furnished as a sitting-room. Indeed, it was commonly used as a drawing-room, the real drawing-room being out of repair and never used. We were received in the hall by a stately old lady, white-haired, but upright as a dart. She was our host's mother. With her was a tall, dark-eyed girl, whose features min com- plexion spoke clearly of a Southern origin. I was not surprised when I learnt that her name was Francesca Riotti. It turned out that she and her widowed mother lived permanently at the Castle. Her father, Count Riotti, had been a great friend of the late Chief, Inchgoil's father, who had offered him and his family an asylum when they fled from Italy after the triumph of Victor Em- manuel. ' Soon after his arrival at Inchgoil the Count died; and his widow and infant daughter stayed on at the Castle, for the very good reason that they had no other home to go to. After a slight refreshment, in which Madeira and the "wine of the country" were offered us in lieu of tea, I retired to my room, while Steyne was con- ducted to Inchgoil's apartment. The dinner-hour came. I went back to the hall, and found that the Countess Riotti, a dark, fine- looking woman of fifty, with a superlatively haughty manner, was with Mrs. Macleod. Her presence kept me silent, and I was glad when the white-haired lady begged me to go and try to break up the conference between her son and Steyne. They had paid no attention, she said, to the dressing-bell. I was guided to a room in the western part of the Castle, and knocked at the door. A voice shouted something, but the door was so thick that I could not distinguish the words. Assuming that I had been told to enter, I opened the door. There sat Steyne, with his back to me, at a square table. On the other side of the table towered a Hercules of a man, in rough homespun, wearing a short brown beard, his face putple with anger. "I tell you, sir, I will not permit it!" he shouted, bringing his fist down upon the table with a shock that would bee broken the bones of any ordinary hand. "If the idiots chatter—let them chatter. • My wife, sir, was an angel in the form of a woman. She died by the will of her Creator. To say that she was poisoned is absurd. Who was there at tliat hotel who could by any possibility have done such a thing? Did I wish her to die? And who else was there in the house that could have had a desire for her to die? No one. But the doctors do not understand liow the death hap- pened? No; and because they are ignorant—God knows they are!—or curious. I am to submit my angel's flesh to their knives. They are to probe and-- Sir, I will not do that thing! /removed the lady's bony to a place of safety, and there it shall remain. No curious, prying surgeon shall ever touch it. I will take the responsibility of my actions. And now, sir "—his manner changed instantly from one of haughty defiance to that of a condescending host—" I fear we must make haste over our dressing. It is close upon dinner-time." "One moment, Incligoil," said Steyne, rising as he spoke, "one trifling question, and I promise you not to refer to this subject again unless you intro- duce it yourself. I was told of a box of pills which was found in Mrs. Macleod's dressing-case. There was no poisonous ingredient in any of them, for they have been analysed. Do you know where she bought those pills? "I believe they were a simple antibilious remedy prescribed by my mother. She compounded them, I believe, with her own hands, and gave them to her just before we left home. I suppose the fiscal does not pretend to suspect that my mothfr is a secret poisoner ? " "Certainly not," said Steyne, with a smile; and having thanked the Chief for answering his question, we both left the room.</p>
        <p>The next four days passed absolutely without in- cident. We were treated, and we behaved, as ordinary guests; the dark mystery to which we owed our presence at the Castle was never mentioned. Steyne spent a good deal of time in talking to the lady of the house. He seemed to take a vast interest in her medical theories and in the remedies she prepared. Sometimes he would be invited by her to watch her operations in the still-room. As for Inchgoil, he rarely appeared except at meals. I tried to talk to the Countess and her daughter; but my arl;ancea wer6 so coldly received that I soon withdrew them. The Countess had a particularly supercilious, disagreeable manner. She seemed to e.0.m.. Far laa;t two nereons in the universe—her</p>
        <p>daughter and herself. No "one erie was it tire slightest importance. Miss Riotti resembled her mother too much for my taste. I found that the world of her thoughts was inhabited by three persons—herself, her mother, and Inchgoil. More than once it struck me, observing the gentleness of the girl's tone in speak- ing to the Highland Hercules, and noticing one or two other little signs, that the Italian girl had a sentimental admiration for the Chief. It seemed to me likely enough th 4 there had been some soft passages between thent at one, time or another; and I thought it probable that the signorina would be very willing to become the Chief's second wife and future Lady of Inchgoil. It was dusk on the evening of the fourth day of our visit, when Steyne came into the hall, where I was sitting alone, and sat down near me. "Farrer," he said abruptly, "I have found out the truth at last. Poor Mrs. Macleod was mur- dered."</p>
        <p>"What do you say ? " I stammered, in a whisper. "Do you think Inchgoil-" "Inchgoil kill his wife? No! No indeed!. But I know that the poor girl was poisoned, and that the poison came from this house." "Hush!" I cried instinctively, glancing around. One of the doors (menhir/ into the hall was aiar. and ititeyne had not even lowered his voice. "Do you mean that you know who did it ? " "I do. The poisoner is in this house at thit moment. She put a pill of arsenic into the little phial of liver pills that. Mrs. Macleod gave to her innerAter-in-law. " - .</p>
        <p>Did my me? Or was there a fainb rustle of a woman's dress behind the half-open door?</p>
        <p>I rose and went towards it; but Steyne detained me a moment, while he caught me by the arm and said:</p>
        <p>"I shall not even tell you her name. But I shall tell the Procurator Fiscal all I know at once, and leave him to act. So get your traps packed to- night. We must take the steamer to-morrow morning." I managed to get away from him, and, stealing to the half-open door, peeped into the room. It was empty; but the room had a second door only a few feet away, so that I could not feel sure that our conversatioh had not been overheard.</p>
        <p>At dinner that night Steyne said to Inchgoil that we must leave in the morning, but told him no more than that. After dinner we did not return to the ball for a cup of tea, as Steyne preferred to go at once to the smoking-room. Spirits and water were produced as usual • and I noticed that Steyne, the only one of the three who took hot water and sugar with his Talisker, took that night only cold water. I handed him the sugar-basin, which contained, I remember, only soft sugar; but he pushed it away. He chatted away perseveringly till about half-past ten, when we all rose to go to bed. The Chief blew out the candles, having first lit his own, and preceded us to the door of the room. Then I saw Steyne in the semi-darkness with one swift motion, empty the contents of the sugar-basin into his pockethandker- chief. As he replaced the sugar-basin on the table he dropped into it a note twisted into a "cocked- hat " shape.</p>
        <p>Of emirs°, I said nothing, but waited in my room, listening to the moaning of the wind and the sullen dashing of the waves on the crags beneath my window, till Steyne should come to me. I felt sure I should see him that , night; and I was not disappointed, Just at midnight he opened my door, and came in. I had extinguished my light, and gone to bed; and Steyne felt his way to the bed and sat down.</p>
        <p>"I was right," he said. "The Countess cares for no one but her daughter; and I knew from Mrs. Macleod that she thought Inchgoil had cast a slur upon her girl when he married the English governess. No doubt she hated the woman who had taken the place she intended for her daughter. I saw that she must be the guilty one, and that she must have administered the poison in the way I told you. But I had no proof, and no way of getting any proof. So I told you in the Counttss's presence the conclusion 1 had come to. Yes, I knew very well that she was in the breakfast-room listening; and I quite expected that she would try to poison me, in order to prevent my denouncing her. As soon as I saw the crushed sugar in the sugar-basin, I thought that would be her plan. She would be sure to know that Inchgoil takes only cold water with whiskey, and, of course, she was bound not to run any risk of killing him; so the substitution of soft sugar for lump sugar seemed suspicious. I took it away, and left a note to say that it would be taken to the public analyst at Inverness. Poor woman! Poor wretch! Will :she try to escape, I wonder' Will she succeed?"</p>
        <p>The Countess did make an attempt to escape. Next morning a small boat was missing; and as the wind had risen during the night, it was at first supposed that the little craft had been torn from her moorings. But a pair of oars had also dis— appeared, and the Countess was not to be found.. Next day- the boat was discovered floating bottom upwards in the bay. The body of the lady was never recovered. Her daughter now resides in a convent in Italy.</p>
        <p>The Procurator Fiscal was satisfied with the evidence Steyne laid before him—the sugar contained arsenic enough to kill half-dozen men—and aban- doned his investigations. The body of the ill- fated English girl rests once more in the lonely churchyard of Kilbarra.</p>
        <p>[Tim END. 1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>RUNNING A NURSING HOME.</head>
        <p>RUNNING A NURSING HOME.</p>
        <p>Few people have any idea of the large amotmt of capital necessary to equip even a small nurs- ing home, and of the financial responsibilities involved in its efficient upkeep, says the Hos- pital. Some idea may be gained by studying the SUMS of money necessary to be raised even to provide an open-air sanatorium. No one in their senses nowadays hires an old house in the centre of London with the design of receiving patients as a charity; nor if they did would the poor be disposed to accept free the faulty ac- commodation which the rich must purchase at a high price. And yet, on the other hand, no one ever dreams of finding a good site for a nursing home, and erecting on it a building approximat- ing in any way to a modern hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ADVICE TO GIRLS.</head>
        <p>ADVICE TO GIRLS.</p>
        <p>The girl who is plain is urged to physical exercise and the cultivation of cheerful thoughts. A sympathetic smile, we are told, may trans- form an otherwise unattractive face; but that smile, declares a New York paper, must go with a quiet voice, a simple -coiffure, and unos- tentatious dress.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CASHMERE FROCK FOR LITTLE GIRL.</head>
        <p>CASHMERE FROCK FOR LITTLE GIRL.</p>
        <p>A pretty, simple, and very becomiag little frock for a small girl forms the subject of our sketch. The material in which it is realised is a fine cashmere in a charming tone of " Gobelins" bhp. The little bodice is merely' a very full affair of the material gathered on either shoulder in a series of gaugings, the gaugings forming a sort of small sloping yoke on each side. Over this corsage comes a sort of bretelle arrangement of the material. These bretelles are furnished with small outstanding epaulettes, both epaulette and</p>
        <p>strap being edged by a narrow binding of velvet in exactly the same shade and a line of machine- stitching. Embroidered buttons fasten the bretelles to the waistband on either side, this Waistband, by the way, being finished on either edge by a binding of velvet to match the bre- telles. The little skirt is full, and is gathered all round the waist, except just in front, where it is arranged in a wide box-pleat. A strapping of the velvet and a line of machine-stitching suit- ably outlines the hem.</p>
