      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>HIGH HEELS NOT SMART.</head>
        <p>HIGH HEELS NOT SMART.</p>
        <p>High heels are rejected by the smart, declares a Globe fashion writer. They are left, to the over-dressed, or the masses. The heel of the moment slopes but slightly forward from the back, and is not more than an inch and a quar- ter in depth. The winter boot and shoe are in shades of brown for preference with walking costume. Patent leather is reserved for visiting or driving dress, and even with these, if they are white or light of tint, the shoes are often in tan or bronze, or pale brown. A purple cloth gown with faded beech-leaf cloth facings and belt looks very well with gloves and shoes exactly matching these. Worn with a felt or velvet hat of the same soft, pale tan colour, trimmed with the immensely fashionable autumn leaves, the whole is very complete.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ELEGANT FROCK.</head>
        <p>ELEGANT FROCK.</p>
        <p>Of simple but most elegant Ind becoming style is the beautiful frock pictured in our illus- tration. A fine light make of cloth in the new "cocoa-colour" is the material in which it is achieved, the trimmings consisting of nothing more than a little satin and some soutache em- broidery in exactly the same shade. The bodice is cut in crossover fashion, and lightly draped round the figure, with just the faintest suspicion of pouch at the waist. From the crossover fronts turn back two immense .collars of the cloth, beautifully embroidered in a handsome soutache</p>
