      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>CI7LT OF INDIVIDUALITY.</head>
        <p>THE CI7LT OF INDIVIDUALITY.</p>
        <p>We often see beautiful women who frankly and ostentatiously make a cult of their beauty. If more ugly women made a cult of their ugliness and more old women a cult of their age, if only women in general would come to the conclusion that it is not necessarily desirable that all should be young, beautiful. and fashionable, individual women would, declares a Paris correspondent, present to the eye much more character and much more interest than they now do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPES.</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPES.</p>
        <p>CRECY Soup.—Melt two ounces of butter or clarified beef dripping in a pan. Prepare four sticks of celery and two onions, and slice them into the pan. Wash and scrape six large carrots; cut off all the red part, and add it. Cut up four ounces of uncooked ham in small pieces, and add also. Put the lid on the pan and allow the con- tents to cook ten minutes; then add some stock and cook until the vegetables are tender. Strain the vegretables and rub throurrh A. sieve. Put them back in tne pan with the stock, and bring to the boil, skimming off all fat. Let it cook gently for ten minutes, and then add a teaspoon- ful of castor sugar, salt and pepper, and s. little cream, if liked, and servo,'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>INFLUENZA SEASON. Some Valuable Hints about a New and Strikingly Effective Treatment.</head>
        <p>THE INFLUENZA SEASON. Some Valuable Hints about a New and Strikingly Effective Treatment.</p>
        <p>Influenza takes so many varying forms with every new outbreak that doctors are often puzzled to recoghise the trouble. Yet there are two points common to all the varying phases of Influenza : first, the microbe which causes the disease is always the same active, virulent, and deadly germ, bred in crowded city slums ; secondly, the gei'm always finds its way into the blood through the throat and lungs -it 'floats about in the air, and is breathed into the system. Just now is the Influenza season of the year, for the heavy clammy air holds and fosters the microbes. First of all you feel as if you had been hard at work for a week without sleep; then your throat begins to ache, and your voice gets hoarse and the throat feels relaxed and painful. Your bead throbs, there is a dull pain behind your eyeballs and at the back of your neck. You are shiveling all over, yet you are all on fire inside.</p>
        <p>With a bex of Peps always handy you can defy the worst epidemic of ifluenza, for the wonderful medicinal fumes which arise as Peps dissolve pleasantly in the mouth, are powerfully antiseptic and germ destroying. Apinst influenza Peps provide the strongest possible defence, for they fortify the throat, lungs, and bronchial tubes with a medicine which goes direct to these vulnerable parts, thus expelling the disease and destroying its cause. Liquid medicines are worse than use- less, since they can only be swallowed into the stomach and cannot possibly reach the lungs, to say nothing of the weakening and relaxing effect of the laudanum, chloral, morphia, and other deadly narcotics these mixtures usually contain. This wanderful breathe-able medicine in solid tablet form is of special value in the treatment of the deadly after-effects of influenza. During that critical period of convalescence after the fever has left you, a regulaz course of Peps will strengthen lungs and bronchial tubes, effectually preventing pneumonia and pleurisy, and checking that well- known "influenza cough" which is often the most obstinate sequel of an attack Gf influenza. Keep Peps always bandy, especially during the influenza season. But beware of worthless sub- stitutes. See the short distinctive title— Peps -- cn the box and on every tablet. Of all chemists, Is. id. and 2s. 9d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>LATCHFORD WARD ELECTION</head>
        <p>LATCHFORD WARD ELECTION</p>
        <p>IVIr. Singleton Returned</p>
        <p>The bye-election in Latchford Ward on Monday was a party fight between the Liberals and Conservatives and aroused considerable interest throughout the• town. Mr. William Bibby was the Liberal candidate and Mr. Henry Singleton opposed him on behalf of the Couserva- tiNes. The day was one of the worst imaginable for electioneering, a thick fog enveloping the town. The workers, however, were most energetic in their efforts to bring the voters to the poll, a large number of vehicles being placed at the disposal of each candidate. There was a great deal of mural literature and, as in the November elections, the Conservatives made a great deal of the education question. It is believed that on this account Mr. Singleton received the Roman Catholic vote, although in his address Mr. Bibby pointed out that as a Churchman he had the highest regard for the people who had built the schools and educated the children in the past and he would not support any serious interference with the present method. A handbill issued on behalf of Mr. Bibby pointed out thathe is a life-long supporter of the party which gave the workingmen the Trades Disputes Bill, protecting their society funds, and their rights as workingmen to combine. Reference was also made to the long services of Mr. Bibby as a Friendly Society worker ; and it was stated that Mr. Singleton entered the Widnes Council as a Liberal. Several meetings had been held in support of Mr. Singleton's candidature, but Mr. Bibby contented himself with canvassing. The polling stations were at Christ Church School and at St. James' School, and the booths were open from 8 a.m. to 8 p.m. On behalf of the Conservatives Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., made a tour of the Ward in the morning. The votes were counted in the Council Chamber in the presence of the Mayor and the return- ing officer (Alderman Evens). The result was :</p>
        <p>Mr. Singleton . 805 Mr. Bibby 457</p>
        <p>Conservative majority ... 348 Mr. SINGLETON proposed a vote of thanks to the Mayor, the returning officer and the officials for the excellent way in which the election had been conducted. He said he bad been returned by a handsome majority, and would try not to disgrace them. He honestly believed that at the end of three years' service they would be perfectly satisfied and want bim to stay with them.</p>
        <p>Mr. BIBBY seconded. He said although he felt some- what disappointed be scarcely expected to win %%ben be considered the forces against him. He congratulated Mr. Singleton on his victory. • The MAYOR said he was pleased to see that the candidates finished on such friendly terms. Alderman EVANS also replied, and afterwards announced the result to a large crowd assembled in font of the Town Hall. CANDIDATES' SPEECHES.</p>
        <p>Addressing the crowd, Councillor SINGLETON said he was extremely obliged for the handsome manner in which he had been retured as the representative for the Latchford Ward. He thanked all those 805 electors who had voted for him. He was quite sure that at the end of three years, those who bad not voted for him that day would be amongst the first to come forward to support him then. (Applause.) Mr. WILLIAM BIBBY also thanked the 457 voters who bad supported him. He was not at all discouraged by the result when he considered the political, religious, and "butchering " bodies which bad been arranged in one mighty element to defeat an ordinary individual like himself, lie was not at all surprised, though he was a little disappointed at the size of the majority. He could, of course, have wished the result different,though he must accept the inevitable. He had been the first in the Town Hall to congratulate Mr. Singleton, and be would be the first to congratulate him pullicly. (Applause.) He hoped he might enjoy good beahh and have a successful career in the Council. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>LOCAL WILL.</head>
        <p>LOCAL WILL.</p>
        <p>Thomas Gerfard, of Kingsbury Cottage, Appleton, Widnes, formerly licensee of the Alexandra Hotel, a member of the Town Council for three years, and chairman of the Widnes and St. Helens Licensed Victuallers' Association has left £6,089.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>LADY OF INCHGOIL.</head>
        <p>THE LADY OF INCHGOIL.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN K. LEYS,</p>
        <p>Author of "In the Grey of the Dawning," "In the Toils," "A Broken Fetter," "A Maiden's Victory," "The Mystery of Loudon Castle," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>On a bleak March day, several years ago, I was standing at the corner of Wellington-street ane the Strand, looking, with lack-lustre eye, at the full flowing river of humanity, and vaguely wondering whether I should be able to scrape together the pence necessary for the night's shelter.</p>
        <p>Yes, I had come down as low asi that. For some days past the shadow of the workhouse had been creeping nearer and nearer to me. The gulf of pauperism, into which many a better man thttn •I has plunged, never to emerge from it, yawned at my feet. -And yet I had committed no crime, no great blunder even. I had simply failed to find work. I was one of the many victims of the old- fashioned delusion that giving a lad a good education is equivalent to teaching him a trade or profession and starting him in business. I was a waif, all but penniless, and very nearly hopeless. "Farrer, old fellow! How are you ? " said a voice at my ear. _</p>
        <p>I started, and turned round. My first impulse was to run away and hide myself. I was ashamed to be seen by my old college friend, himself well- dressed, clear-eyed, and successful. "Come, now; don't say you don't remember me," said Steyne, as for the moment I did not answer him. 'lt's not so very many years since we dined together in the common-room. . But I've really not a second to spare. Come and dine with 'me to-night-17, Brussels-square, Bloomsbury. I won't take a refusal. Come just as you are—no ceremony. Seven, sharp. You will? That's right! Au revoir, then." He nodded to the driver of a passing hansom, jumped in, and speedily disappeared. That meeting was for me the beginning of a iiew epoch.</p>
        <p>I spent the whole of my available capital in making myself as presentable ail could; and seven o'clock found me mounting the steps of No. 17, Brussels-square. After dinner my old schoolfellow told me that he was a solicitor, and that his practice was in the West-end of London, and consisted chiefly in advising in difficult and intricate cases for clients who preferred to confide in one man, and objected to their private affairs being made the talk of an officeful of clerks. He added that he had plenty of business, and would have liked to take in a partner had such a course been possible. "Tell you what, though," cried Steyne, "there's nothing to hinder my having an assistant; and you shall be my assistant, Farrer, if you have no better billet in view."</p>
        <p>No better billet in view! and I had paced the streets all the cold, dark hours only the week before! My eyes filled with tears. It seemed as though Paradise were about to open before me; and, indeed, there is a greater difference between a tramp and a respectable clerk than there is between a clerk and a millionaire.</p>
        <p>I entered upon my new duties next day, and found my work intensely interesting. Many of the cases which were brought to the office were real tragedies, enacted, as it were, under our eyes; and of these I have selected one—not the least striking —for my present purpose. Steyne made a point of having me present, if possible, during his first interviews with clients, so that I might know the facts at first hand from the beginning. One evening, just as I was about to leave the office for the night, the whistle of Steyne's speak- ing-tube sounded, and I at once obeyed the sum- mons. Two ladies were with him—fresh clients-- standing together in the middle of the room. One was a short, pale-faced creature, in widgw's garments—evidently the sheep-dog. The other was a tall, plain-featured, high-bred-looking woman, clad in Scotch tweeds. When she spoke it was with a marked Scotch accent, which had nothing vulgar about it, and in a peculiar, deep, resonant voice many tones below the usual feminine standard.</p>
        <p>"I am come to you at this hour because I am in trouble, and the matter brooks no delay," said she. Steyne bowed and handed her a chair. She seated herself, and proceeded with her narrative in the same restrained, precise terms as if she had been delivering a scientific lecture. "I am the wife of Colonel Douglas Farquhar," she said, "and the sister of Duncan Macleod of Inchgoil, which, I daresay ye know, is one of the Outer Hebrides. My 'brother lived there happily, with my mother and the rest of the household, till last year, when a family we knew on the island of Lewis engaged an English governess for their chil- dren. The young woman was comely, and, I suppose, desolate at being so far from home. How it, came about I do not know; but my brother made her an offer, which she accepted immediately. It was, from every point of view, a most unsuitable match for him. They were married about six months ago, and travelled on the Continent for a time. Then they came home; but my sister-in-law had taken a dislike to Inchgoil—a ten-roomed villa at Putney would have suited her better—and made my brother promise that she should spend part of each year in London. However, before the time came to carry out the promise they were parted. They—that is my brother and his wife, for my mother is too old to travel in winter—went to visit some friends on the mainland after the New Year; and last month, as they were staying at an hotel at Portree, waiting for a steamer to bring them home, the poor young thing was suddenly taken ill, and died.</p>
        <p>"The doctor did not rightly know what to call her complaint; and it seems that, after the young wife was laid to her rest in the kirkyard at Kil- barra, a foolish claver was got up that she had not come naturally by her death. Some even hinted that Inchgoil himself, my brother, that was so fond of her, compassed her destruction-" The lady stopped abruptly, clearly because her emotion was about to master her. But she gave na other sign of feeling. In a minute or so she went on as before.</p>
        <p>"One of Mrs. Macleod's relatives, a rich London tradesman, heard of these rumours; and the Pro- curator Fiscal took the matter up. He got a war- rant to take up the body and examine it, to see if there were any traces of poison; but when he and his men went to the vault in Kilbarra Churchyard the body was gone." "The body was gone!" echoed Steyne. "Ay," said the lady, "coffin and all. There was no doubt about that. The Fiscal was furious, and swore he would get a warrant to 'search the Castle—as if he was likely to find anything there! —and my brother. threatened to cool the Fise,al's blood in the waters o' the Little Minch. The Chief has a terrible temper when he's roused—he might commit a murder of that kind, I allow. And ColorA Douglas Farquhar and I are very anxious that he should have with him some man of sense, who could give him a word of advice, and maybe some guidance. There seem to be some things that require explanation, too, in this case,"</p>
        <p>c‘l quite agree with you, madam," said Steyne drily. "But why do you not employ a Scotch lawyer ? " "It's not a lawyer that's wanted," said the lady impatiently, "but a man of gumption—l mean, a man of sense. You were recommended to me as having a gift of seeing through a stone wall that is most uncommon. Will you try to see through thi. one? Will you try to persuade and guide my un- happy brother ? " Steyne did not answer for a few moments; and the lady rose, as if to go. "I will do what I can," he said at last. "Can you start by the ten o'clock express to- night? If you could do that you would have time to break your journey at Inverness and see the Fiscal—if he has gone home, as I daresay he has by this time—and yet be at Strome Ferry in time to catch the steamer."</p>
        <p>"Very well. I will start to-night. I should like, however, to take my secretary with me." "There can be no objection to that," said the 1.4,7 " A e.Art vow.. fee and expenses----4</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>PREMTURE DECAY.</head>
