treated in of the year taken alone—and a very fatal quarter this way. of the year it often is—the rate at Huddersfield was I reduced to 62 which means in that one quarter a I saving of 26 lives. I close with some extracts from a letter which Mr. Broadbent has been good enough to send me :—" Gatesgarth, Lindley, Huddersfield, November 16th 1907. Dear sir,—l forward a budget of papers in relation to infant mortality. We have been working at this for the past five years and it is some- what surprising that only now has it come about that the general public is being touched by the horrible and needless waste of the lives of and health of little help- less babies. In this Christian England they are being sacrificed not to Moloch but to ignorance, which is more cruel, at the rate of very nearly a hundred a day. Yet with this fact well known by everyone who pays any attention to what has been published again and again the public conscience is only beginning to be touched. You know that it was these little babies that our Master took in his arms and how for once he was angry when they wished to keep them from Him. What pain and grief it must be to Him that we His followers of to-day place so slight a value on these little ones that every day we kill or allow to die a hundred of them. Please excuse these hasty vehement words. I have studied the question closely and I know that all this is the statement of bare facts and it can all be remedied at once if we wish.—Yours most sincerely, BENJAMIN BROADBENT."</p>
        <p>SWEETS FOR THE CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Nearly all children, especially if normally healthy, crave for sweets. A great many parents, without any thought or reason in the matter, deny to their children all kinds of sweets. They do this from some preconceived notion that sugar and candy cakes are bad for the children. Other parents go to the opposite extreme, and indulge their children in all sorts of confectionery. from the cheapest to the most expensive, allowing them to eat rich, indigestible cakes, jams, can- died fruits, &amp;c. Give the children sweets in the form of pure chocolate, honey, and syrup made from fruits. A lump of sugar or a stick of good • candy now and then will not hurt them. Let them eat molasses, but be sure it is a good quality. Fruit jellies, if unadulterated, and plain cookies that are not too sweet are good for chil- dren. Let the children have sweets. The sys- tem craves them. They impart warmth and energy. They nourish and build up the tissues. The best time to give the children sweets is at meal time. Let fruits, jelly, syrup, molasses, or honey form part of each meal, and then the children will not so often plead for candy and cake. Let the children have sweets. But see to it that they are furnished with the proper kind, at the right time, and in sensible! quantity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>One of the biggest League battles of the season is down fur decision at Wilderspoor to day and there should be an exhilarating exhibition between Warrington and Wigan. There has always been keen iivalry between the clubs, but it was never keener ihail in the last few years. As a rule the results of the two matches between Warrington and Wigan settle the destination of the South-West Lancashire Cup, which each club has held twice. This season the odds are on Wigan, unless Warrington do them the honour of beating them to-day, and again on February 15th.</p>
        <p>On the season's record Wigan are a better team than Warrington at present, but there should be little in it to-day and that little in favour of Warrington. It is not often that Wigan have taken points away from Wilderspool, and the locals should just about compel them to bow the knee again to-day if there are no more last minute changes in the composition of the team. Neither Isherwood nor Fish is fit yet—in fact the latter seemed very stodgy on Saturday—but the following strong side has been selected:—</p>
        <p>Tilley, full back ; Brooks, Taylor, Dickinson, and Kenyon threequarters ; Lees and Jenkins halfbacks; Thomas, Boardman, Shugars, P. Boardman, Belton and Heath forwards.</p>
        <p>Wigan also expect to be at full strength and the team for to-day is : Sharrock ; Leytham, Jenkins, Tom Thomas, Miller; Gleave, J. Thomas; Blears, Silcock, Wilcock, Cheat- ham, Brooks, and Ramsdale.</p>
        <p>Six points are at stake in the Wilderspool match altogether, viz. 2 each in the Northern, Lancashire, and South West Lancashire Leagues.</p>
        <p>The Notthern League table up to-date is as follows For.</p>
        <p>A gat. Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. c'tige. Oldham Himslet Wigan lO 8 1 1 25 31 152 12 11 57 85.60 Keighley lO 7 3 0 23 21 109 15 12 66 70.00 Batley l2 8 4 0 29 29 145 18 19 93 66.66 Broughton Barrow Warrington Hull ll 7 4 025 27 131 17 13 73 6363 Hull K. R. Halifax Wakefield T ll 6 4 1 27 30 144 19 20 98 5909 Salford Dewsbury ll 5 6 0 15 18 84 li3 19 93 45.45 Leeds Bramley Bradford lO 4 6 0 16 17 83 15 25 105 4000 Leigh York Runcorn St. Helens l2 3 8 1 14 19 85 20 50 190 2916 Widnes ll 2 7 2 10 21 83 21 29 119 2545 Swinton Rochdale ll 3 8 0 11 17 83 25 24 122 2515 Ebbw Vale ll 2 8 1 8 10 46 20 40 160 22.71 Huddersfield Merthyr</p>
        <p>The following is the Lancashire League,to date : For Agst.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. P. G. T. PtS. G. T. PtS. Oldham 6 6 0 0 12 15 26 108 4 4 20 Warrington Wigan Barrow 8 5 3 010 918 72 614 54 Salford 7 4 2 1 9 13 17 77 10 10 50 Leigh Broughton Widnes Runcorn Swinton St. Helens Rochdale</p>
        <p>The South West Lancashire Competition stands as follows : Points.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Avt. Wigan 3... 2... 0... 1 37 -. 7 _. 5 Warriugton _ 4 .- 2 -. 1 _ . 1 . _ 51 -. 13 .- 5 Leigh St. Helens 3 -. 2 -. 1 ._ 0 29 -. 34 _. 4 Widnes - 2 -. 2 -. 39 -. 45 -. 4 Runcorn 5 -. 1 - . 4 _. 0 -. 25 -. 52 -. 2</p>
        <p>A noticeable feature of this season's football is the drop in scoring aggregates. Last season, with the reduction to 13 aside and other alterations, heavy- scoring was the rule rather than the exception. For instance, Warrington started off by scoring 30 points or over in each of five successive home matches, and scores well up in the thirties wire quite common. New defensive measures were necessary under the changed conditions and those measures have now been perfected. Not only have the All Blacks found this out, but scoring everywhere has fallen to a normal level though the game is faster than ever. Northern Union football is rapidly approaching perfection, and the visit of the tourists with their original ideas should do good. They may be beaten occasionally but they can teach us one or two things in tactics.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>INFANTILE MORTALITY.</head>
        <p>INFANTILE MORTALITY.</p>
        <p>REV. S. PITT AND THE COUNCIL'S R ESPONSIBILITY.</p>
        <p>The Key to the Problem.</p>
        <p>The Rev. S. Pitt preached 'in tbesubject of "Infantile mortality" at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Sunday evening. He took for his text the words : "Take heed that ye despise not one of these little ones for I say unto you that in heaven their angels do alway behold the face of my father which is in heaven" (Matt. 18, 10); "And whoso shall receive one such little child in my name receiveth me" (Matt. 18, 5.) He said: A few weeks ago I ventured to say from this pulpit that there were certain things in our present social conditions which it was impossible to defend and which Christianity had nothing to do with except to be ashamed of and to alter them as soon as possible. At the same time I hinted that one of these things was the condition of child life especially in parts of our great towns. To this matter I propose to refer more particularly. Needless to say that it is directly con- nected with " the appalling extent and dire results of intemperance" to which according to direction of Con- ference special reference is to be made to-day. It is a dark and terrible subject. Since I have looked into it it has become a burden from which I would gladly be free but it is the burden of the Lord and it must be borne. The facts lam about to lay before you are by no means new. They have slumbered in reports and statistical articles. It is high time that they were brought to the attention of the Christian congregations of this town. The bare fact is then that the INFANTILE DEATH RATE IN WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>stands at 157 per thousand. That is that .for every thousand children born 157 die within the twelvemonth. The case is bad enough in the country as a whole but in comparision with the rest of the country and even with other towns Warrington occupies a bad eminence. The figures are for England and Wales 133, for 76 great towns 145, for 142 smaller towns 138 for England and Wales less the 218 towns 116 —Warrington again 157. In one district of Warrington however the case is much worse. There is one ward in which we have the terrible figure of 241 per thousand. The births in the whole area were 2,248 ; the deaths under the twelvemonth were 353. In the St. Johns' Ward the births were 378 and the deaths under the twelvemonth 81—almost one in four. There is nothing in East London to equal it and here it is at our doors. Our Councillors, and especially the Health Committee, has had a heavy responsibility for these things for a long time past. Everyone of us that has a vote in the Borough has a responsibility for it now. As a back ground to this state of things we give the fact that in the village of Villiers le Buo in France the infantile death rate has been eliminated altogether. The Mayor of that village who is also a doctor has introduced and carried into effect such regulations that every child born during the ten years period 1894, 1903 was alive at the end of it. This is no doubt a state of things too good to be hoped for in a community like ours but no one who hears me to the end can doubt that large reduction of the present tragical figures is possible. I know that there is</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CYNICAL HEELING</head>
        <p>A CYNICAL HEELING</p>
        <p>that lies half unconscious in the minds of many that after all this does not very much matter. They think it in a dim careless way if they do notfsay it, there are , plenty of babies in the world and a few score or a few hundred more or leas of them does not need concern us. Such a thought as that can only live in the dim sub- conscious part of out minds. To bring it out and utter it is to show its repulsiveness. A healthy young life is the most valuable asset of the state and the community. It is an asset that is not likely to be too plentiful the way things are going. Behind all this there looms the danger that our imperial race may degenerate and die away, and that the splendid history of the Englishman may end in shame like the effete, luxurious and cruel empires of the list. But whatever the political economist may say to the Christian the case does not admit of argument. Our Lord directly identifies him- self with the cause of these helpless little ones and declares that , their interests are peculiarly and narrowly watched in heaven "In heaven their angels do alwrpr behold the face of my father which is in heaven. To come back however to the figures the most sinister aspect of the case is the contrast between the various wards of the borough itself : Town Hall, 134; Whitecross, 110; Bewsey, 168; Orford, 161 • St. John's, 214; Howley, 188; Fairfield, 12¢ • St.Awhin's, 113 ; Latchford 159. Several of these contrasts are quite sufficiently startling. I select one ' which is on all accbunts the most significant viz., that between Whitecioss and St. John's. In the one case 345 children are born and 38 die—a little over 10 per cent.—in the other 378 are born and 81 die—nearly 25 per cent. Where does the difference come in? No doubt the drink has to do with it. If anyone will walk through the two wards and notice the number and character of the licensed houses in and around each, he will see that here is one great and terrible factor, but we shall mislead outselves and discredit our- selves before the public if we harp upon that string to the neglect of every other. There are other things than the drink and a good many others concerned in this. Neither is it sufficient to say that the character of the people in general or the character of the mother in particular makes the difference. No doubt the motherly sensibility dogs become blunted in the con- ditions under which our poorest mothers have to live. But God who hath made of one. blood all nations of men that dwell upon the earth has made of one blood the mother who brings forth her child in the slum,where 25 per cent. of the babies die, and in the comfortable I cottage or sweet country village where only about 10 per cent. of them die. The DISIFEREN'CE BETWEEN ST. JOHN'S AND THE WHITECROSS WARDS</p>
        <p>is not a difference of nature but a difference of conditions and surroundings including as one of the conditions a lamentable ignorance as to the proper feeding of little children. Hear what your health officer I has to say: "St. John's has some very bad slums and yards and alleys innumerable. Its population contalns a very considerable proportion of labourers and persons ; having a precarious means of livelihood." it has a birth rate almost as high as Whitecrosst the general death rate,the zymotic death rate and the infantile death rate are each about double. The phthisis death rate is also higher. Here we have the whole thing in a horrible nutshell—overcrowding, poverty, ignorance' and drink producing as their result death. My friends we do not like Socialism but can we be surprised at Socialism while things like this remain as they are? The pro- ' posals of Socialism most to be dreaded are those that threaten the family life but while things like these remain to be pointed at as a shame and scandal to our Christianity and a blot upon the present I state of things can we wonder that proposals of the moat drastic kind obtain a hearing? Depend upon it if we do not settle some of these things within the next twenty or thirty years Socialism will. On the other hand, if we will but follow the little child the child ! will lead us into the very heart of our social system, so that while we are performing the one simple duty of , saving the child's life we shall carry the healing I forces of Christianity into the very places where they • Meanwhile what about Warrington's most recent ; are most wanted and afford an evidence of the truth and I performance. It was certainly not gaudy, but then ! power of the Christian religion which will do more to t no one expected them to win at Broughton unless they I convince the present generation than argument of any, ' other kind. But it is needless, it is hardly respectful had all the luck. To only lose by 8 points to 3 points I , to Christian men and women to labour the point. Surely was not discreditable. The atrocious weather reduced 1 all that can possibly remain for us is what can be done the game to a football farce, and orthodox play was , and we may be thankful that quite out of the question. Warrington might have i A CLEAR LEAD won with more speed on the wings, for both men had has been given us by the action that has recently been chances, but really there was little to choose between ' taken in Huddersfield and in Wakefield, but particularly the respective backs on either side. All tried hard 'in Huddersfield. There dwells at Longwood near and all made occasional blunders. Broughton had an ' Huddersfield a certain Mr. Broadbent, brother to Sir advantage forward and this just about turned the , William Broadbent, and a good Methodist whose name scale in their favour. Tilley and Jenkins both dis- , occurs twice over ift our Missionary report as a sub- scriber. This gentleman was recently Mayor of Hudders- . toguished themselves on the Warrington side. Lees field and he bethought him to spend a hundred pounds did little beyond score the only point, and lam afraid during the year of Mayoralty to see if something could that he is making the mistake of thinking that when be done in this matter of infantile death. Never was a he has got the ball away he has done his share. A Mayor's money better spent as the results will shew. little more aggressiveness on the part of the "skipper" Accordingly he offered a sovereign for every child born would be appreciated. during his year of office who should be alive and well at ; the age of twelve months. Mr. Broadbent indignantly • disclaims the idea that the sovereign was offered in</p>
        <p>The All Blacks went down on Saturday for the fourth time but it was defeat with honour. To only lose by a point to the undefeated Oldhamers on their own ground was a moral victory, and from what I hear the least bit of luck would have turned the scale in favour of the New Zealanders. They gave a remarkable display under the worst possible condi- tions, and for once in a way their forwards more than held their own. Oldham had anything but the easy win they anticipated and .were thankful when "time" was called. The All Blacks play much more serious football in Saturday matches than in Wednesday matches.</p>
        <p>Isherwood's benefit match has been decided upon. The popular threequarter id to have the proceeds of the Broughton Rangers match. at Wilderspool oil January 25th. Another footballer to whom a benefit is being given byis club is W. Briers, the well-known St. Helens forward.</p>
        <p>The New Zealanders were nilled for the first time, at Runcorn on Wednesday, while the Linnets ran in three tries as the result of smart football. The All I Blacks played such a poor game that it is difficult to avoid the conclusion that they are devoting their eiaeraies to the Saturday matches, and playing any- anyhow in the mid-week engagements. If Warrington - can get a representative team together by December 21 they should justify their title as cup-holders at the expense of the All Blacks. To-day they play Dewsbury and Batley combined, and ou Wednesday next are due at Swinton.</p>
        <p>Warrington's chances for to.:day's match are some- what lessened by the fact that Kenyon's knee is not yet sound and he will have to stand down from the selected side. Jordan will probably fill the vacancy. Unless Fish has done some bard training he can scarcely be considered a candidate.</p>
        <p>Warrington" A. "successfully accounted for the Rangers A" on Saturday, but they have a sterner task before them at Wigan to-day. Nevertheless with the team available they should stand a fair chance of capturing the points. Should the Warrington lads win they will be almost assured of the Combination Championship. The team for Wigan is: Hewitt, full back; Fish, Cartwright, Landor, and Harrison, threequarters ; Lloyd and Daintith, half backs , Forster, Naylor, Jolley, Morley,Holbrook and Preston; forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>VARICOSE VEINS VANQUISHED.</head>
        <p>VARICOSE VEINS VANQUISHED.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk Completely Cures a Severe Case at Liverpool.</p>
        <p>There are few more distressing physical derangements than varicose veins, which are capable of serious developments. So severe are some cases that even skilled medical men are unable to give the sufferer any relief,but in such extreme cases the victims are asked not to abandon hope. Let them be guided by the experi- ence of Mrs. Eliza Bartlett, who lives at 16, Coniston- street, Everton, Liverpool. At different periods during the past eighteen years Mrs. Bartlett has been a martyr to this disease, and though doctors have been defied, a simple home remedy which is now in almost every household brought her the rslief which she had for so long looked for in vain. Mrs. Bartlett, who is a middle-aged woman, and the mother of ten children, recently recited the details of her wonderful recovery to a reporter who called upon her. "Eighteen years ego" she said, "before my third child was born I was troubled with varicose veins. I was for four months confined to my bed and a doctor attended me every morning and applied dressings of iodine. I gradeally improved until the birth of the next baby when the varicose veins appeared again above my left ankle. I was troubled in the same way prior to all my subsequent confinements, but the worst attack was four years ago. I went to a doctor and afterwards to the dispensary, but I gained no -relief. The vein , stood out just like an egg, and I had to be carried about the house. But worse was to come. About_two months ago a small sore broke out at the side of one of the veins on the shin bone. The sore gradually grew deeper and I spread across the leg nntil it was an inch deep. The doctors appeared to do me no good, and my eldest son who was employed at a wholesale chemist's in Liver- pool said that by the huge sale of Zam-Buk he was sure lit was a good thing, and advised me to give it a trial. He wrote for a sample, but before it came my leg caused me such agony that I bought a box of Zam-Buk. The effect was marvellous. The first dressing with Zam-Buk brought me relief. The tightness of the skin relaxed and the pain ceased, while the sore gradually disappeared altogether. Now, lam glad to say that the , vein has gone ever so much smaller, and the other varicose vein in the leg has quite gone. Zara-Bak has done wonders for me, and, thanks to its marvellous ; healing power, I have no pain now. I can get about ! better than I have done for years."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Smart Penalties for Street Betting.</head>
        <p>Smart Penalties for Street Betting.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Saturday, William Parker, Ashton-street, and Joseph Grestey, Winwick- street, were charged with frequenting Market-street on Thursday and Friday for the purpose of betting. Mr. Warburton, solicitor, represented Parker.—lt was stated that Constables Woollacott and Reddington kept observation in the neighbourhood on the days mentioned They saw the two defendants at various times receive slips of paper. When arrested a number of bettiug slips relating to Manchester races were found on both of them. In order to avoid the vigilance of the police scouts were posted at different places. Defendants were watched by means of field glasses. Supetintendent Monaghan said that he had known Parker since his boyhood, and during that time he had scarcely done anything else, but betting, having been twice recently convicted. The magistrates fined Parker £2O and ccsts, and Grestey £lO and costs.</p>
