        <p>WIDNES. £5, £7, £lO, .£l. £3O, £5O, £lOO. ADVANCES MADE PRIVATELY to TRADES. MEN, FARMERS, WORKINGMEIN, and all Respect tie Householders. on their own security, without delay, on our simple written oromise to pay balk. FAIR AND HONOURABLE DEALINGS. U. COWEN, VICTORIA CHAMBERS, WIDNR- TOOT OF THE CENTRit STATION. MANAGER: JOHN H. GOOD WIN. N.B.—Warrington and Runcorn Apolkant may receive Money any Evening at their own Homes.</p>
        <p>1/ousito anb lanb on Avail..</p>
        <p>The November Cabinet TEE NEXT meetings are over, and the SESSION. PRIME MINISTER has gone</p>
        <p>away for his well-earned rest. The bills to be introduced in the next session of Parliament may be presumed to have the final form so far as the nature of their pro- posals is concerned, whatever changes may be made in the drafting. Ministers know what each of them is to endeavour to do during what promises to be a Parliamentary session of much more than average interest, as it will be of more than average duration. The two Houses will meet a fortnight earlier than is usual, and it seems to be the present intention to prolong the session through the autumn, after an interval for rest and sport from about the end of July. The programme will be correspondingly large There is to be a new Education Bill, and an Old- Age Pensions Bill. These will be measures dealing with great and burning questions. Any one of them would a few years ago have given importance to any Parliamentary programme into which it was introduced. But there are many other bills to be produced on the Ministerial Front, one of them of the rank of Mr. BIRRELL'S Irish University Bill. The Irish Secretary is believed to have dis- covered a method of settlement which may be expected to be fairly satisfactory to all parties con- cerned. Should this actually prove to be the case he will be reckoned henceforth as undoubtedly a great Parliamentarian. That the Licensing Bill will be of a thorough character was evident from the speeches made the other day by Mr. ASQUITH to the three deputa- tions that waited upon him. One of these bodies was afraid of confiscation. It represented the interests of the holders of debentures secured upon breweries and licensed properties. These were the most sanguine of all investors not long since, and now they appear to be nearly the most depressed. Such depreciation had been going on during the last few years, said Lard FABER in their behalf, that their ordinary and pre- ference stock has fallen from £11,500,000 to £5,000,000. It is a tremendous fall, without question ; but it could not have taken place if the price of public-house property had not been most unreasonably it&amp; end. The property in security is the same. Nothing of that has Linen away. All that has happened is that in estimating what it is worth it has been written down to something like its actual value. Mr. BALFOUR'S legislation was to have greatly increased the value of public- house property. That was the presumption on which it was based. When one licence was extinguished another became worth more money in the market, and therefore, could affard to pay a share of the compensation given to the dis- possessed owners. Matters have not turned out according to expectation, as is almost always the case with legislation based on the determination to benefit classes at the expense of the community. Mr. BALFOUR did not even succeed in helping his friends.</p>
        <p>What seems certain is that the coming Licens- ing Bill will embody one of the chief of Lard PEEL'S proposals. The present system of com- pensation, that is to say, will be replaced after an inteival, say of seven years, by something resembl- ing the system which was in existence before Mr. BALFOUR'S ill-judged interference, which presumed that licences were the properry of the public, and loft them to the discretion of the magistrates. The magisterial jurisdiction, which was cer- tainly never oppressive, will probably be re-established in full. Something, it seems likely, will be done in regard to tied houses, and, undoubtedly, in regard to tied clubs, of which there is an increasing number. Oa° of the deputa- tions which waited on the CHANCELLOR or THE EXCHEQUER represented Conservative and Liberal Clubs, which, it was contended, are excellently and lawfully Conducted ; but the clubs Mr. ASQUITH had in his mind do not appear to have been political. They are the new social clubs,</p>
      </div>
