        <p>generally in country or working-Class districts. Many of these are admirably conducted, and there la no reason whatever for interfering with them but some, there seems little reason to doubt, exist for drinking and nothing else. They tend to drift secretly into the hands of the brewers, and to reproduce the tied-house system in a new and Worse form. In regard to these the Government teems to have some drastic proposals in hand.</p>
        <p>like brothers. If the spirit which animates the service is that which ran through the whole of Nelson's fleet, the desire to do only the nation's work with the materials at hand, we shall hear little more of the jealousies ani bicketings which have been so much and so unfortunately canvassed of late. Much depends upon those in high com- mand. Theirs it is to set the standard by tact and restraint. A high tone in high places always carries its complement of loyalty in the lower ranks. The nation supplies the means to win battles, but the 03:1ity to do so depends up In the officers, and the greatest fac`or in sncces is harmony of action and intention.</p>
        <p>------ There was a very thick _ LitresFoßD in Latchford on Monday, to ELECTION. which . may, perhaps, be ; attributed the downfall of I 'the Liberal candidate, Mr. W. Bibby. The electors I 'seem to have been befogged and obfuscated by the NOTES AND NOTIONS. Specious protestations of Tories and C ttholics united in the holy bonds of religious education. What connection there is between this qnestion In reporting one of the speeches after Monday's and the work of the Mimicipal Cotancillor we fail ' election, our "neutral" contemporary credits Coun- to see; tbo Town C avicil does not determine the cillor Fulton-Smith with saying "they had brought nature of religions instruction to he given in the their candidate to the top of the pole." If it was a elementary schools. Liberals might just as greasy ding down again ere long.pole we may i expect the candidate to reasonably insist on a pledge from Conservative h come o 0 * * municipal candAates in 'favour of the continuation i tion . The Workingmen's Committee of the Warrington . of our Free Trade p )licy. The religions educa . ' li Since to b° question is being used solely as a political District Nursing Association have every reason red herring, and we are surprised that our one- pleased with the result of their first year's work. time Catholic friends should allow themselves t_2o b May NW they have raised over £3OO towards this H led by the nose in such a simple fashion. How 9 . valuable institution and the enthusiasm which was long, we wonder, will this hastily-form alliance ' manifested at the annual meeting on Thursday gives between Tories and Catholics lant ? Just so long promise of greater things in the future. ed as the Tories fiad their new friends useful and I Wesleyanism is progressing in Latchford and the docile I Meanwhile the government of the town new Sunday school opened on Thursday is undoubtedly is being given over to a party which is financed one for the best-adapted in the district. It speaks by the money of the brewers—a party whieh is much of the self-sacrifice of this working class con- obviously pledged to consider the selfish and , gregation that the scheme has been brought to a mercenary demands of " the trade" in preference , successful issue and they are justly proud of their to the welfare of the community. We had given commodious and beautiful buildings. the Catholics credit for more insight, but they seem to be blinded by religious prejudices There are now hardly any souvenirs of Napoleon I. Which cannot fail to bring about an unpleasant at St. Helena. The tomb no longer exists, and the reaction sooner or later, house which is shown to tourists to-day is not the one -- where he died. The real house ended by being a While there was no mis pig-sty. At the utmost there still exists at St. Helena understanding the temper : a willow tree planted by Napoleon, but that is not THE CoNG 1 the meet- certain.—" Petit Parisien." ATROCITIES. and earnestness of ing called on Wednesday to I * 4, * The Rev.. T.. H.. Harris, who has been on the Congo and seen with. his own eyes the atrocities he vouches condemn the atrocities perpetrated on the Condo, ' the resolution saPPorti"9: for, was practically given the lie direct at Wednesday's firm action by the Government was not carried with the unanimity b W ' - , meeting y arrington Catholics who developed a anticipated. There were a few dissentients—anctdden sudden „ tenderness for the reputation of the Catholic they not only dissented from the purpose of the King Leopold. Explanations and eridenee are wanted Meeting, but they questioned the bona-fides of the j before we can accept at this belated hour the state- agitation which is now proceeding apace from one Iment of Catholics even so respected as Father Wilson end of the country to the other. They actually I that the Congo agitation is founded on a fabric of disputed the reality of the inhuman barbarities in fabrications. the Congo district under King Leopold 's' control, We should have thought the evidence of the R iv. J. H. Harris and other missionaries—the veracity of which is sworn to—would havebeen unimpeach- able, therefore it was an amazing thing when the rt ev . Father Wilson mounted the platform and said " they had not had put before them such evidence as he believed any magistrates in the Country would convict a lad of the pettiest of petty larcenies upon." The rev, gentleman was sup- ported by other Catholics in the same astounding attitude of doubting the whole genesis of the agitation. In effect these gentlemen, after seeing Photographs and hearing details of the most horrible atrocities—all of which are vouched for and accepted without question by some of the highest and most respected 'people in the land— these gentlemen of Warrington say there are no atrocities I It is a unique attitude to adept, and while we respect Father Wilson's protest, we cannot do other than Condemn in the strongest possible manner his ingenious special pleading on behalf of King Leopold, who is univers illy execrated as an arch- villain. We note, too, that the " Catholic Times" of yesterday takes up the same position as the Warrington Catholics. Surely we may be pardoned for questioning inotives,if these gentlemen dispute the veracity of !nen like the Rev. J. H. Harris ! Is there any significance, with respect to the Sudden espousal of King Leopold's cause on such Slender grounds, in the fact that he is also a Catholic, and an enormously wealthy one too? Father Wilson quite failed to justify his action on Wednesd.y, and if he has a case to state wo should he glad to affo-d him the facility of our columns.</p>
        <p>Our kind-hearted Mayor has given a leal in a worthy cause which we hope will be quickly fol- lowed by other prominent townsmen. His Worship has offered to subscribe 20 towards the reduction of the nOCI debt on the Nursing Association on condition that fifteen gentlemen will each contribute a like amount. Already promises of two other f,2o's have been forthcoming and it is to be hoped the Mayor's generous example will provoke others to good works, with the result that the debt will be extinguished.</p>
        <p>_ Silver-street school serves one of the poorest dis- tricts in Warrington but on visiting it on Tuesday, on the occasion of the presentation of the prire won in the rounders competition, one could not but be impressed with the bright and happy faces of the youngsters. They entered into the proceedings with the greatest gusto and their loud cheers fairly shook the rafters. Hearty congratulations must be accorded to the girls on their splendid achievement in winning the Championship of the League for the first year of its existence.</p>
        <p>Our sympathies are with Mr. W.Bibby. He fought a gallant fight in Latchford on behalf of Liberalism, but found the combination of Conservatives, Catholics, and butchers too much for him. Latchford has reverted almost entirely to Toryism again. Its representatives are Councillors Pemberton, Wilson, and Singleton. Even the former is understood to be a Conservative, but as he does not put Conservatism before everything else he is not beloved of his party. Even one independent man in Latchford is better than none.</p>
        <p>* The Catholic Young Men's Club was the scene of great rejoicings at the victory of Mr. H. Singleton in Latchford Ward. As the victor is a Catholic this was only natural, but the presence of Conservatives like Councillors W. B. Roberts, W. J. Wils,m, and J. Fulton-Stnith was significant. These ardent Tories suited themselves to the occasion by speaking in fervent terms of the faith of the parents &amp;c., and the need for Conservatives and Catholics to stand shoulder to shoulder. Perhaps they spoke with their tongues in their cheeks. There will not be many Conservative Catholic candidates, notwithstanding the joyful cele- brations at Mr. Singleton's success.</p>
        <p>• We hope the Rev. Sidney DCFANTILE Pitt's sermon on infantile MORTALITY. mortality, which we publi;sh in another column, will do something to impress the public with the serious- ness of the question. The extent to which the child life of the c suntry and even of our own town is being sacrified through parental ignorance and unhealthy conditions is truly appalling. Mr. Pitt ightly directed attention to the local aspect. To nee his own words, Warrington " occapies a bad 'eminence," for statistics show that of every 1,000 children horn 157 die within twelve months, as compared with 133, the rate for England arid Wales, and 145 for the 76 great towns. The startling contrast between St, John's Ward, whelk) according to the Medieal Officer's report, there are some very bad slums and yards and alleys innumer- able, and Whitecross Ward, the healthiest part of the town, was strongly emphasized by the Preacher. Whereas in St. John's the infantile death rate reaches the alarming figure of 214. in Whitecross it is only 110. W 0 believe Mr. Pitt eort.sctly attributes the -distressing state of affairs in the first-aamed district to overcrowding, poverty, ignorance and drink. Surely it. is time we bestirred ourselves to -Mitigate this awful " sacrifice of the innocents " which is one of the greatest reproaches of our time. But what is to we d.)ne? It will he readily conceded that ignorance on the part of mothers is one of the primary causes, and the sooner organind efforts are pit forth to dispel it, the better. Mr. Pitt produced convincing evidence cf the headway which has been made in this directien in Huddersfield where, through the co-operation of municipal activity and Private effort, the infantile mortality has boon very appreciably reduced. In that town there is a Strong voluntary association known as the Public Realth Union which is worked by upwards of a hundred ladies and addresses itself to the task of Visiting homes and giving anuad advice acid help. Of course its activities are welcomed and hacked up by the authorities anl the results so for are cause for thankfulnesq. Cannot slmothing similar he Promoted in Warrinton ? Too Indly-disposed could not set their hands to a nobler work than the protection of chi'd life and the co-operatioe on the part of the municioality should not be lacking. Probably Councillor Evans will have something to say on the question in his promised statement on the health of the town at the C3ancil meeting tlext Tuesday.</p>
        <p>Many a smile maketh a flirt.—" Mizner." A. fool and his words are soon parted.—"Shenstone." We can only be valued as we make ourselves vain- able.—" Emerson." _ _</p>
        <p>—Worry, which is unbelieving, labour, has slain its thousands—F. W. Farquhar." _</p>
        <p>If so-called good people had the energy, the nerve, the backbone of so-called bad people, the bad would be trampled out of existence. —" Mrs. Craigie." The object of education is not to make machines, but persons.—" Paul Janet." The lives of most are ruled by the fatal error that the more one possesses the more one enjoys.—" Paul Sabatier." _</p>
        <p>Unless we have the sense of justice abroad, like a common air, there's no peace, and no steady advance. —" George Meredith." • Mr. W. E. Harvey, M.P. for North-East Derbyshire will speak at the Co-operative Hall on Wednesday evening, when we hope there will be a fine gathering of Labour men, Liberals and Progressives of all shades. Our readers will recollect the convincing address Ur. Harvey gave in Parr Hall on February 18th last, and will doubtless be delighted to hear him again. At that time Mr. Harvey had only just risen from a sick-bed, aud his self-sacrifice was warmly appreciated. Since then he has been very busily engaged owing to the unfortunate illness of his mining colleague, Mr. Haslam, \l P. for Chesterfield. The work of looking after both divisions in addition to that connected with the Derbyshire Miners' Associa- tion has fallen on Mr. Harvey's shouiders, so that he is putting himself to no small inconvenience in coming to speak at Warrington on Wednesday next. The Derbyshire Miners' Association is the most snocessful and wealthiest miners' organisation in the country, and it has been built up almost entirely by the efforts of these two gentlemen, both of whom now have the honour of representing the miners in Parliament. I Since he was so fortunate as to he retnrned unopposed on the Ist instant, Councillor Fulton-Smith has become very militant. He bestrides the town like a veritable Colossus • the Education Committee, the Conservatives, and the Junior Catholics how down to I him. On Monday he uttered some words of wisdom at the Catholic Young Men's Club. After blowing the froth off—a somewhat vulgar allusion which will I perhaps not be deemed inappropriate—the substance of Councillor Fulton-Smith's remarks is as follows : "It was very desirable that they should introduce into the Town Council a better feeling among the members. Before long he hoped they would be able to silence the noisy party which had done so much to get it such an unpleasant reputation during the last two or three years. It was a pity the Council should be ruled by men outside the Council, who had got minions inside. The sooner they were entirely free from them the better it would be for the town and its reputation, and the better for its financial condition." .</p>
        <p>-- Lc,rd Charles Beresford I COMRADESHIP IN mercifully refrained in his I THE NAVY. speech to the Salters' Com- pany from dealing with a certain incident which ' has lately set many irresponsible tongues wagging, and has done prob- ably more harm than its author contemplated. , At the end, however, of his very interesting review of naval history in the last half-century he essayed to lay down the principle that for the success of the Navy it is necessary that the spirit of com• radeship should animate the officers. The sea- service, perhaps, puts more upon the relations of than to man than any other walk in life. Those who spend their life on the narrow compass of s ship are thrown continually into one another's company. lii p!eaFant weather when Work is slack they must turn to each Other for comradeship. They cannot escape from surroundings that have become !moon. genial by going into the next street to seek new friends, or by joining another club. It is this constant and inevitable companionship which makes men tolerant of each other, and enables them to work together in harmony in times of stress and strain, :in!i which helm hO oater, too, When he comes lo rin•lte sele.itions for nay Ocular tasks to put his lit,nfls upon the right man among the comrades whom he knows and can trust. Lord Charles Beresford pointed to the xample of Nelson and his officers, who ware de3cribed in language by no means exaggerated as being 'LINSEED COMPOUND' for Coughs and Colds. Relieves Asthma and difficult breathing.—Advt.</p>
        <p>This might be construed without much difficulty into an attack on the Non-Traders —" the noisy party." It is fortunate they made the noise they did, or we should be paying rates at 10s. in the £ to-day. Perhaps that would be the "better financial condition" Coun- cillor Smith speaks about. But who are "the minions ?" Do they come from the Conservative Club, the Breweries, or whence? And the men" out- side the Council " who " rule the Council !" Who are these puissant people ? The speaker might have belled the cat before letting it out of the bag !</p>
        <p>For Chronic Chest Complaint, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Why do Johnston Watson's Eye-glasses and Spectacles produce such a delightful soothing sensation to tired and aching eyes? His clients say" Because the correct lens is skilfully adapted to each eye."—JOHNSTON WATSON, Eyesight and Spectacle Specialist, 84, Bridge-street, Warrington. Low charges. Satisfaction guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
