        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 30, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 30, 1907.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>THE CONGO ATROCITIES. His Worship the Mayor (Alderman S. Owens) pre- DEATH UNDER CHLOROFORM. sided over a well-attended meeting in the Co-operative Hall on Tuesday. to protest against the crying evils Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at St. perpetuated upon the Congo natives. The Chairman Helens on Monday into the death of Richard Mayor, was snpported on the platform by the Right Hon. Lord fifty-two, a collier, of Pendlebury Cottage, Sumber- Monkswell, the Rev. J. H. Harris, returned Congo lane, Collins green. Mayor died at the St. Helens missionary, the Revs. J. Wright Williams, Douglas, E. Hospital on Thursday while being operated on by Drs. Morton, Stephen Jones, N. S. Poole, T. Baker, and A. Cotton and Latham. Deceased had been treated for Harvey, many members of the Town Council, and several years, off and on, by Dr. Valentine, of Earles- prominent local townsmen.—Alderman Owens said they town, for hernia, and he on Thursday last decided that 1 were assembled in the cause of humanity. From Africa an operation was immediately necessary.—Dr. Cotton comes a cry of great distress. In that country atrocious said that the operation was started in the usual way, crimes had been done, and are still going on, worse than after the condition of the man's heart, &amp;c., had been even in the dark days of Bulgaria. The horror of these considered, but very soon after chloroform was doings was appalling, and they welcomed Lord Monks- ; administered Mayor collapsed. The operation bad not well and Mr. Hbrris to enlighten them on the subject. ! actually been entered upon, and the man died.—The —Lord Monkswell and the Rev. J. H. Harris delivered ! Coroner said the percentage of deaths of people under lengthy addresses, and enumerated many instances of , chloroform was very small.—Dr. Cotton said that was shocking treatment meted out to the natives by the 1 so.—A verdict of "Death from misadventure" was Belgian Government's rubber hunters.—A resolution , returned. was moved by Councillor F. Neil and enthusiastically I supported.—A collection was afterwards made on behalf ' of the funds of the Congo Reform Association. ' WIDNES MURDZR CHARGE. PRISONER COMMITTED TO ASSIZES. At Widnes Police Court on Tuesday, John Byrne, a chemical labourer, living, until his arrest, in Brown- street, was ,charged with the wilful murder of his wife, Rose Ann Byrne, on Sunday, November 3.—On Tuesday Mr. Knowles appeared on behalf of the Public Prose- cutor, and Mr. Peters defended. Several witnesses testified to Byrne and his wife having quarrelled on the evening of November 2. Dr. Eustace said the cause of death was a fractured skull, but the woman's injuries were not caused by a fall but by a blow from a blunt metal instrument of convex shape, probably a hammer • the woman could never have come through the window-opening. After a hearing which last seven and a half hours, the prisoner was committed for trial at Liverpool Assizes. Large crowds awaited the result outside the court. RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. A meeting of this Council was held at Dutton Work- house on Tuesday afternoon, under the presidency of Mr. G. Pilling. There were also present :—Messrs. T. Wright, W. M. Morris, J. H. Davies, J. Hallows, A. Haining, T. Baxter, J. Spruce. A. H. Talbot, J. B. ChaOwick, P. Booth, G. Fllgertou-Warburtrn, T. Antwis, H. Mawdsley, J. Bolshaw, E. Batch, A. Broome, E. Bretherton, G. F. Ashton (Clerk), Dr. Adams (medical officer of health), W. Diggle, J. Swinton (surveyors), and J. Farrington (inspector of nuisances).</p>
        <p>ALLEGED EMBEZZLEMENT AT ST. HELENS William Schofield, manager, and Alexander Young, shopman, who had been employed at the Donegal Tweed Company's establishment in Cburch-street,were remanded at St. Helens, on Monday, on charges of embezzlement. Mr. E. W. Swift said there were a great number of tharges that would have to be investigated, and he asked for a week's remand. Schofield was alleged to have embezzled £2 2s. 6d. and Young 10s., but there wasanother man named Scholeyin custody at Liverpool. There seemed to be no doubt that the three had been guilty of conspiracy, and the case could only be properly tried when they were all together.—Detective Roe said he arrested the men on Saturday night, when Young said," This thing has been coming on for some time. It was going on whcn I came be the shop first, and T fell into the trap. I suppose I shall have to pay for it." Later he said," If we have defrauded people it has been to the advantage ef the company as well as ourselves, because to some extent we have credited the company with part of the money." Schofield also made a stutement.—A remand was granted, with an offer of bail in £2.0 and two sureties of £lO each.</p>
        <p>_ LOANS. A letter was read from the Local Government Board sanctioning tho borrowing of £3,080 for the parish of Stockton Heath for the purposes of private street improvements.</p>
        <p>ROADMEN'S SIOURS. MR. HALLOWS AND SOCIALISM. Mr. T. Wright said that no doubt the members of the Council knew that the roadmen in the employ of the Council left off work at three o'clock on Saturday after- noons. They went to dinner at twelve o'clock and got back about one, and he considered it a waste of time for the men to come back and work till three. He moved that the roadmen be allowed to finish work at one o'clock. Mr. HALLOWS said he had great pleasure indeed in seconding the resolution, and considered it a step in the right direction. Justice tempered with consideration would stop the mad Socialism that was going about. It would make no difference to the farmers. The present arrangement was simply a waste of time. Mr. Spruce moved as an amendment that the matter be referred to the Highways Committee. Mr. J. H. Davies seconded and Mr. Haining supported the amendment. On the vote being taken Mr. Hallows said he was in favour of the amendment as well as the resolution (Laughter.) The amendment was carried. ALKALI WORKS INSPECTION'. Mr. T. Wright said he together with Mr. Piling attended a meeting held at Liverpool to consider the appointment of a sanitary inspector to inspect the Alkali Works in Widnes and Runcorn area. Warrington had decided to give £25 and Runcorn £2O towards the expenses but when Warrington fonr.d out that Runcorn were giving £2O they were sorry they had decided to give £25. It was, however, decided to appoint an additional inspector. The report was adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
