      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>GLASSMAKER'S TERRIRLE SUICIDE..</head>
        <p>GLASSMAKER'S TERRIRLE SUICIDE..</p>
        <p>The inquest on Samuel Dean, aged thirty-five, a glass:. maker, of 112, Albion-street, was held ou Monday at St. He!ens Town Hall.—Mr. Brighouse said it was reported to him that for about six months Dean had been very strange in his manner. On one occasion he went away from home, and the same night his wife received a letter in which he told her that he was going to make away with himself. He returned home at the end of three days, when Dr. Latham advised his removal to the imbecile ward at the workhouse. He improved, and was liberated at the end of a fortnight. He next com- plained of pains in the head, and that he could not sleep. On Thursday night he left home at half-past eleven, evidently intending to go to his work at Pilkington's Sheetglass Works. He did not do this, but was after- wards seen at the Globe Chemical Works, warming him- self in front of a kiln. He left there about half-past five on Friday morning. His movements from that time could not be traced until noon, when John Gillespie, a signalman at the Pock Nook Box, saw him lying between the metals on the main line from St. Helens to Wiga.n. His head had been cut off by a coal train that was being shunted.—Police-constable Whalley said Dean's clothing was saturated with water, and he must have been in the canal. Rain could not have drenched him so completely.—A verdict of "Suicide whilst insane " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>HAYDOCK COTTAGERS' GARDENS.</head>
        <p>HAYDOCK COTTAGERS' GARDENS.</p>
        <p>A large gathering assembled on Saturday night at the Haydock Colliery Schools, when, after a tea parry, Mrs. C. R. Pilkington distributed a number of ambulance certificates and the prizes in the cottage gardens com- petition. The gardens competition covers St. Helens, Haydock, and Earlestown to Golborne. In the St. Helens district Police Constable John gall was first for the best front garden, with Alfred Davies second, and R. Dickson third. Best hack garden: 1, R. Brown ; 2, J. Hull ; 3, John Hall. Best hedge : 1, 11Irs. Derricatt ; 2, T. Frith ; 3, J. Hull ; 4, A. Trail. Pewfall district.-Best flower garden: 1, S. Eden ; 2, A. Stainer: 3, J. Platt. Back garden: 1, E. Edwards: 2, T. Ashton: 3, J. Houghton. Best hedge: 1. J. Dearden ; 2, J. Houghton; 3, J. Unsworth ; 4, T. Ashton. Clipsley-lane-Best garden front: 1, E. Baines ; 2, A. Davies ; 3, H. Winstanley. Back garden: 1, S. Steele; 2, E. Baines; 3, J. Britch. Best hedge: 1, S. Steele; 2, J. Britch ; 3, A, Reid; 4, W. Ranson. Church-road :-Best front garden: 1, L. Mather; 2, R. Lee 3, A, Chalmers. Back garden: 1, J. Anders; 2, I'. Heaton; 3, Lot Bolton. Best fence: 1. T. Heaton, J. Anders; 2, J. Strong: 3, P. Bolton. New Boston-Front garden: 1, Mrs. Wilde; 2, J. Rigby; c, J. Whittle. Back garden: 1, H. Wilcock ; 2, H. Lentz ; 3, W. Pilling. Best hedge : 1, J. Bond; 2, M. Heyes ; 3, C. Click; 4, T. Lee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>LABOUR MEETING AT ST. HELEN'S.</head>
        <p>LABOUR MEETING AT ST. HELEN'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Glover, Labour M.P., for St. Helens, addressed a meeting on Tuesday in the Scholes-lane, Thatto Heath. Mr. 'S. Butler presided, and was supported by Councillor Barton Fletcher, Mr. H. Twist, Mr. Waring, and others.—Mr. Glover spoke of the work of the Labour party in the House of Commons during the past session. He said the first efforts of his party were devoted to carrying out their elcction promises, and the first fruits of their labours was the restriction of Chinese labour in South Africa. First of all licences were stopped and the work of deportation was begun, and in about two years there would no longer be any Chinamen in South Africa. With regard to old-age pensions. he -said that the Labour party would give the Government no rest until some scheme of old-age pensions was granted. They had already got Mr. Asquith to ear-mark the sum of £2,500,000 as toe nucleus of a scheme of old-age pensions. The Labour party were determined that, when an aged person was in recespt of a pension, it must be free from the taint of pauperism, and his vote must not be taken from him on that account. With regard to the unemployed question, he said it was the duty of the Government to find work for a man who is willing to work but cannot get it. One great triumph for Ile Labour party was the securing of a trades union wage for Government employees. The present Workmen's Compensation Act as it stood at present, was the result of the action of the Labour party, who had moved amendments to it, which had made the Bill so favourable to the working classes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN FOREIGN MISSIONARY ANNIVERSARY.— The Rev. Alfred S. Sharp, who has been pioneer mission- ary in Bechuanaland, Rhodesia, and other parts of South Africa, paid his third visit to Padgate on Sunday to preach at, the foreign missionary anniversary at the Wesleyan Church. His discourse on Sunday morning was based on the words, " Brethren, pray for us that the word of God may have free course, run and be glorified." In the evening he spoke from the parable of the Good Samaritan. In the afternoon a juvenile missionary meeting was held. when Mr. Sharp told an interesting story of his experiences. On Monday evening the annual missionary meeting was held, and was presided over by Mr. Fred Wright, in the unavoidable absence of the advertised chairman, Mr. J. E. Richardson, owing to illness. The Rev. W. Mellor read the report, from which it appeared that the sum of £4O had been contributed last year by the Padget° Wesleyan Church to the missionary cause, this being an increase of £2 on the former year. Mr. Sharp took as his subject `• The joys and sorrows of a missionary's life," and detailed his experiences in the Transvaal. When Mr. Sharp wet to Islafeking he found a church of 400 members, but when he left there were 1,200 members--a growth of about 100 members per year. He concluded by illustrating from his own knowledge and experience the many open doors in that country for the spread of the Gospel, especially among the young. Mr. Sharp was thanked most heartily for his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>ST. ANDREW'S DAY.</head>
        <p>ST. ANDREW'S DAY.</p>
        <p>Caledonian Association's Dinner.</p>
        <p>To-day is St. Andrew's Day, but local Scotsmen kept the anniversary of their patron saint last evening, when the nineteenth annual dinner in connection with the Warrington Caledonian Association was held at the Lion Hotel. A company of about seventy assembled, Councillor Fulton-Smith presiding. An excellent repast was served by Host Lee, the dishes including that prime favourite " Haggis." Afterwards the usual toast list was gone throngh. The loyal toasts were pfoposed by the President and enthusiastically honoured. The Vice-President (Mr. L.Davenport) proposed" The Navy, Army and Reserve Forces" which was responded to by Major P. M. Appleton. rhe toast of the evening —" Land 0' Cakes " was entrusted to Mr. A. J. Jolley whose speech was well chosen and interesting. Mr. Robert Pierpoint submitted " The town and trade of Warrington," and the Mayor responded, Mr. William Skinner proposed " Our patron ;" Mr. George Bowman, "Our guests ;" and Mr. J. A. McGregor "The President." During the evening musical items were given by Miss Harrison and a quartette party consisting of Messrs. J. L. Davenport, E. Whitehouse, F. Scatter- good and L. Aukland. kfr. W. H. Payton, Mus. BAC. accompanied. The occasion was not allowed to pass without selections on the bagpipes, Sergeant Donald Murray, of Liverpool City Police, an expert piper, being in attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Coming Events For coming events of importance readers are invited to study the announcements an the front page of this paper.</p>
