      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman. Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MR. PARKER AND MONOPOLY.</head>
        <p>MR. PARKER AND MONOPOLY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln his latest letter Mr. Parker would have us believe that only the land monopoly stands in the way of the railway worker getting the " full rAward of his labour."</p>
        <p>In this connexion it is interesting to compare the statement of Mr. Parker with that of a well-known Liberal M.P., and noted political economist, Mr. Chiozza-Money. Mr. Money says" Another illustration is to be found in railway stocks, many of which have (1) been deliberately watered, and (2) risen in price on the market, so that, while railway men are badly paid, the present holders of the stocks are apparently making small profits. Many railway companies have enlarged their ordinary_ capital by the delightfully simple process of multiply- ing by two. £lOO of ordinary stock has been changed into £lOO of "preferred " and £lOO of "deferred." This has not been done behind the scenes, but boldly and with the permission of our rich men's Parliament. As a consequence it is made to appear that the nett receipts of railways are only , about 31 per cent of their "paid up" capitals. But the nominal capitals have not been " paid-up" ; and even in so far as the original capital is concerned much of it is unreal. Thus the magnitude of the injustic which they suffer is hidden from the railway s.3rvants. They risk their lives for the public every day and what do they get for it? In 1904, the 27 leading railway companies paid only £29,009,000, in wages. These 27 companies own nearly all the railway lines and make nearly all the profits assessed by Inland Revenue Commissioners. These profits amount to nearly £40,000,000 per annum or far more than is paid in wages in one of the most dangerous and most useful of all occupations.'r F. A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>CRITIC AND "THE VOICE."</head>
        <p>THE CRITIC AND "THE VOICE."</p>
        <p>Sir,—"Currentis " in your last issue takes exception to the title which is given to this column ; thinks it a misnomer, and infers that "The Voice of Socialism " would he more representative. He then goes on to suggest that a new party beformed to include all trade unionists, who aim at practical reform, such as those who are members of the Conservative Labour Party and the Progressive Labour League, and the name of this party should be the " Actionist Labour Party." When one reads a letter like this, he is struck with the simplicity and ignorance (or is it insincerity?) which is abroad in the twentieth century. Why should not " Socialism " be discussed in the " Labour " columns of a newspaper? he Labour Party in Parliament, is composed of Socialists as well as trade unionists, and they work amicably together for "the immediate " improvement in the life of the working man. Regarding the new party proposition. What a brilliant idea, and how strange no genius ever thought of this before ! ! And the name suggested is the "Actionist Labour Party." Surely a narty constituted from the ranks of the Conservative Party and the Progressive Labour League and given this name would be miscalled. The name to give to such a party, and one that would be truly representative is the "Inactive Labour Party" or the "Anti-Socialist Society." Your correspondent seems to be greatly perturbed and annoyed because the Independent Labour Party is, in his opinion wrongly-named ; but I take it from his letter that he is either a member of the Progressive Labour League, or the Conservative Labour Party, and that he does not consider either of these wrongly- named. Both these parties fight for Liberal and Tory principles ; are financed by Liberal and Tory capitalists; play the tune which these gentlemen call; and when election time comes along they are expected to do their best to deceive the workingman. The Independent Labour Party is not miscalled. At least 95 per cent. of its members are workingmen, and it has never tried to hide its belief in Socialism. The man who goes to its meetings (and there are a good many held in practically every town in the country) and comes away ignorant of the fact that it advocates Socialism, is not, to say the least, very intelligent. As stated before in these columns, we hare an ideal, but so have the Christians that are worthy of the name; and because these cannot be realized at the present time, are they to be thrown aside and forgotten? Soeialists are practical, although they have an ideal. They are going to fight next session for "Old-age Pensions" and an "Unemployed Bill" and if " Currentis " wishes the workers of this country well, I would advise him to come along with us to the half- way house, and then perhaps the vision will be clearer; to take his stand alongside the men of his own class —if he be a worker —who are not so well- armed and entrenched for the battle of life as the Capitalists ()Mae Liberal and Tory Parties. _ _</p>
        <p>- E C. TRADES COUNCIL'S MASTERLY INACTIVITY. Sir, —As I am somwhat interested in the various phases cf the Labour movement I never fail to read the " Examiner" reports of the monthly meetings of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council. Having had a little experience of the splendid work accomplished by the persistent efforts of the organised trades unionists in several other large manufacturing centres, I fearlessly assert that the delegates compris- ing the [present local Trades Council are (by com- parison with those persons occupying similar official positions in most Lancashire towns) sadly lacking in ' initiative; and almost completely fail at all to realise their great responsibilities (as recognised Labour leaders), for they certainly make no serious or sus- tained attempts to obtain any material improvement- of the discreditably inhuman social and industrial conditions under which many thousands of the working classes of Warrington are, alas, forced to exist. Let us take for example their meeting of the 12th instant, which was, I claim, a fairly typical example of the kind of business that has been transacted at these stately gatherings during the present year. After the newly-appointed Chairman had opened the proceedings with the remarks usually served upon these special occasions, and the balance sheet (its presentation had been adjourned the previons month) was adopted, we read that after about half an hour's discussion the National Association of Prudential Agents were permitted to become affiliated with the Council, and a similar "honour' (save the mark) was conferred on the Caretakers' Union. This apparently concluded the important business, and the delegates then dispersed, knowing that they would probably not be called upon to perform the next act in this odd Gilbertian Comedy until Tuesday, December 10th. Good heavens, John! It is ridiculous enough to make the very angels shriek with uncontrollable laughter ! Ton my word! Just imagine for a single moment the exceptionally comic spectacle heie pro- duced by such a group of ornamental, highly respect- able, self-sacrificing intellectual giants thus con- gregated in solemn conclave, and with more or less reluctance, actually consenting to admit to their future deliberations the Caretakers' and Insurance Agents representatives who will be expected (as a matter of course, I presume) to play but a" small " part in this huge screaming farce .of -` masterly inactivity." In all seriousness, Sir, I claim that the rank and file of the local branch of the Boilermakers' Union have ample justification for their recent refusal to become affiliated with so inept and useless a body, and I am not at all surprised when I hear members of other trade union organisations enquiring (as they 1 often do) what practical return do they (as workers) receive from their official connection with the War- rington Trades and Labour Council. The Trade Union movement here is in a parlous condition and Warrington is at least ten years behind most other densely populated centres in Labour politics generally. There is at present not one elected Trade Union representative on any local governing body and a programme or policy has not yet b2en formulated or even outlined as a guide for any prospective Labour candidates for municipal or other honours. Although the Trades Council delegates meet monthly they have not given to their following a strong definite lead on "one burning public question. The smoke nuisance, the grave and steadily iccreasing social menace due to the paralysing and debasing influence of the liquor traffic in our town's pclities,and the complicated problems of education unemployment and housing are all in their parochial-minded estima- tion unworthy ef intelligent discussion, whilst they evidently take not the slightest notice of the arrogant and continuous violations of the Factory A.ots and other workshop legislation by several large firms in this district. Apparently the only-useful purposea that are served</p>
      </div>
