      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>MR. CROSFIELD'S WEDDING. CEREMONIES IN PARIS ON MONDAY.</head>
        <p>MR. CROSFIELD'S WEDDING. CEREMONIES IN PARIS ON MONDAY.</p>
        <p>The marriage of Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., to Afiss Elliadi of Southport and Smyrna, will take place on Monday in Paris. We understand that there will be tin ee ceremonies, including Greek, and English, the first of which will take place in the Greek Church in Rue de Gassen. Miss Elliadi has been in Paris for some time in preparation for the wedding, and Mr. Crosfield left London for the Hotel Castigli,me, Paris, on Wed- nesday afternoon.</p>
        <p>We are sure all our readers will wish the Member and his future wife all possible happiness—a wish in which we c zdially join.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>"The Assassin."</p>
        <p>This is a sensational play on original lines and its many highly dramatic situations appeal to the audience. The scene is laid in Russia and the air is heavy with plots,intrigues and hints of anarchy. The fact that "the assassin" is a woman - Countess Wanda —at once stamps the plot as being out of the ordinary. As presented by Mr. Augustine Knight's very capable company, the play has proved extremely popular and large audiences have been enthusiastic in tbeir appreciation of the efforts of the performers. Miss Ella Vane is fully equal to the heavy demands of such a trying partAlL that of Countess Wanda and presents a finished Tredy. Mr. Augustine Knight as the unscrupulous Prince Demetri could hardly be improved upon and his powerful version of this unenviable role contributes in a large measure to the success of the production. The duel between Countess Wanda and Prince Demetri is a most realistic and exciting encounter which "brings down the house." The play is beautifully mounted.</p>
        <p>AM MACLAR AS CLAUDIAN.</p>
        <p>With a company numbering over 50 artistes appear- ing at the Court Theatre next week in a revival of that celebrated play of the late Wilson Barrett's, " Claudian,' Messrs. Johnson and Potter are certain to have a busy week at the box office. Ur. and Mrs. William Maclaren, who are responsible for the produc- tion, will stage the play nith all the Wilson Barrett scenery, costumes special properties, uniforms' &amp;c., including the Earthquake scene which caused a great sensation when the play was originally produced at the Princess Theatre, Llndon. In this scene play- goers will actually witness the destruction of Claudian's beautiful Palace with its massive arches and pillars. In the title role Mr. William Maclaren will appear. This popular actor may be remembered f r his performance of" David G-arrick" and he has also appeared in " Proof " " Lady of Lyons " &amp;c. He will have the support of Miss Lily C. Bandmann, who comes of a well-known theatrical stock, being the talented daughter of that famous actress, Mrs. B md- mann -Pal mer.</p>
