      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>DISTRICT NURSES. workingmen'sSupport of the</head>
        <p>THE DISTRICT NURSES. workingmen'sSupport of the</p>
        <p>Association</p>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S OFFER.</p>
        <p>The Workingmen's Committee of the Warrington District Nursing Association has already justified its existence, and a satisfactory year's work was under review at the first annual meeting held at the Town Hall on Thursday evening. The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst)presided over a good attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WORK OF THE YEAR.</head>
        <p>THE WORK OF THE YEAR.</p>
        <p>The SECRETARY, in his report, said that the delegates now on the committee numbered 63, the number of works represented being 27. It was to be regretted that several works were still unnpresented, and the Committee hoped that before the end of the year all the works in the district would become affiliated. An effort had been made to induce the trade unions and friendly societies-to contribute, nod it was hoped the efforts in that airection would bear fruit. The balance sheet showed that the amount to be handed over to the Association as the result of the Committee's efforts from May 1903 to June 1907 was £343 7s. 2d. It was pointed out that the Association is about £3OO in debt.,</p>
        <p>The report and balance sheet were adopted. THE MA.YoR'S GENEROUS OFFER.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR in a short address warmly eulogised the work of the Association and generously offered to give £2O towards liquidating _tho debt of £3OO on the Nursing Association on condition that fifteen other entlernen contributed_ a similar amount. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the delegates present should form a general committee with power to add to their number. The following offlcer3 were elected :—President, Mr. W. P. Rylands ; vice-presidents, the Mayor, Captain Crosfield, Councillors Nield and Barton, Messrs. F. Monks, F. W. Monks, Henry Roberts, Edwin Woods, C. E. Bunch, T. Atherton, Willilm Carson, Walter Milner, T. W. Fletcher, J. A. Ransoms and Robert Chadvviok ; chairman, Mr. George Walker; deputy chairman, Mr. T. Wadsworth ; treasurer, Mr. H. Hope; secretary,Mr. J. Fargher ; representatives on the Executive Com- mittee of the Nursing Association, the chairman, secretary and treasurer. Mr. H. ROBERTS also spoke and concluded by accept- ing the Mayor's challenge to become one of the" noble fifteen."</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he had received another promise of £2O from Aldermln Forshaw.</p>
        <p>Votes of thanks were Recoiled to the officers for their ! past services, to tho lady superintendent and staff, and I to the Mayor for presiding.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL MEETING OF THE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the District Association was held last evening in the Co-operative Hall, Mr. F. Monks presiding. In their report the Committee stated that the past year had been one of many changes. Miss Reeve who came as Superintendent in September last had severed her connection with the Queen Victoria's Jubilee Insti- tute, so had been obliged to leave them after, being there only ten months, also several of the nurses who were with them last year hart resigned and had been replaced to ths satisfaction of the Coramitte. The new Superintendent, Miss Turnsll, entered upon iler duties on July Ist and the Committee were looking forward to more settlei times.._ _ _</p>
        <p>esua@fr. F. Monks) in submitting the accounts regretted to report a steady falling off in subscriptions owing to death and removals. The sub- orriptions from the Workingmen's Committee shewed a considerable increase upon former years. The loss on the year was Ss. 11d. against £36 18s. 3d. last year, making the total debt due to the bank £3OO Bs. sd. and it was still increasing. The Superintendent's report showed that the total number of cases nursed was 545, the visits Paid totalling 15,264. The officers of the Association were all re-elected, with the addition to the Committee of Mr. H. Roberta and Dr. Pox, jun. Dr. CATHERINE CHISHOLM, of Manchester, delivered au interesting address.</p>
      </div>
