      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>DAILY BATH.</head>
        <p>THE DAILY BATH.</p>
        <p>People who are too ill or too weak to wash themselves should be bathed every morning, and if this is skilfully done the process soon becomes a great• pleasure to the patient instead of a terri- fying experience; but nothing is more uncom- fortable than to have the nurse leave the face or .arm half dried, when she suddenly bethinks- her- •self she needs another towel, or a little more hot water, or some other thing whi3h she should have -thought of before she began. So that the thoughtful caretaker will see that she has every- thing she requires before she begins the opera- tion of bathing the patient—a basin of hot water, as hot as can be borne by the hand, for it will cool off before it reaches the patient; an -additional can of hot water in case this gets cold: sponge, soap, wash-rag, sufficient towels, warming by the heater or the fir place in cold weather, and a clean night-dress. A little alcohol, to be rubbed on quickly with the palm of thc hand, and some cooling powder are very 'accept- able to the patient, and help to take away the "damp feeling" consequent unon the ministra- tions of another.</p>
        <p>A USEFUL HINT.</p>
        <p>Castor oil and cod-liver oil will not taste so .bad if the child first holds a few drops of vinegar or lemon juice in its mouth. The spoon should eso be dipped in vinegar or lemon juice before ti,e oil is put in. This will not spoil the effects of the oil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>TO CURE NAIL-BITING.</head>
        <p>TO CURE NAIL-BITING.</p>
        <p>Nail-biting is not always a wilful habit to be itured by some punishment, but sometimes an in- Aication of the first stage of nervous degenera- ton. The habit should be treated as a disease. If, affects the general health, as the pieces of •sharp nail are swallowed, and may cause appen- dicitis. A little bitter aloes rubbed on ele finger •*:ips will cure it in children.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>DONT'S ON DRESg.</head>
        <p>DONT'S ON DRESg.</p>
        <p>Don't expect your hair to shine unless you 'brush it well every night and morning. Don't buy cheap boots or gloves. Don't wear your walking dress in the house. Don't use a cheap, poor ribbon in millinery. Don't send anything to the laundry before it is marked.</p>
        <p>Don't on any account put a dress away with- out brushing it. Don't forget to iron the wrinkles out of sleeves occasionally. _</p>
        <p>Don't let boot; and shoes wear through be. fore they are soled. Don't take a bodice off and put it away im- mediately—lay it out to air. Don't hang a skirt up by the fastening—fm two tapes to it for this purpose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>FRUIT DIET.</head>
        <p>A FRUIT DIET.</p>
        <p>The acids and salts found in fruits and vege. tables are the natural antiseptics of the blood, and they are especially of value in summer on account of their cooling properties. Every class of fruit is of value in both these respects. though in varying proportions. Taken the year around, the apple is the queen of fruits from a medicinal point of view. The malic acid which it contains is of equal value to that found in other fruits, aLid this fact in connection with the ease with which it can be procured in nearly every zone and every climate makes the apple of special merit. They may be eaten fearlessly .at any time, especially at night one apple eaten on retirement cools the blood and induces sleep. As many as six or seven apples may be eaten through the day. If this were more ordinarily the case there would be less sickness and °.general debility during the warm • season than</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>HEA ACHE</head>
        <p>HEA ACHE</p>
        <p>And CONSTIPATION</p>
        <p>MOTHER SEIGEL'S SYRUP CURES INDIGESTION, BILIOUS- NESS, CONSTIPATION, HEADACHE, WIND; PALPITATION, LOSS OF</p>
        <p>APPETITE, DIZZINESS,</p>
        <p>MOTHER Mother Seigel's Syrup ARE is now also prepared In Tablet Form CURED SEICEL9S and sold under the name of Mother Seigel's Syrup BY Tablets. Price 2 9 bottle. SYRUP.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Personal. The Rev. F. Clifton Smith, M.A , formerly Vicar of Daresbury, has resigned the living of Ince, near Chester.</p>
        <p>Mr. Birrell's Visit Postponed We learn that Mr. Augustine Birrell, Chief Secre- tary for Ireland, has been c( mpelled to • postpone his visit to Warrington. The meeting arranged for December -18th will, therefore' not be held. Mr. Bitrell has given his word that he will speak at Warrington at the earliest possible opportunity. Wycliffe P.S.A. Owing to the wintry weather the \attendance at the Wycliffe meeting on Sunday was not as large as usual. The Secretary (Mr. B. Farrar) presided. Mr. Hill, of Manchester, gave an address upon "Christian Socialism," Miss Woodland sang in a very sweet manner, "0 Divine Redeemer " and" The children's home," Mr. Payton presided at the organ. Mr. J. Garner read the lesson, and the Chairman offered special prayer f( r the sick member s of the brotherhood. 'Alleged Theft from the Conservative Club. At the Borough Police Court on Monday a young man named Fred Cooke, last from Manchester, was charged with stealing from the lobby of the Conserva- tive Club at 7.30 on Saturday night an overcoat, a pair of gloves, and a muffler' valued at £1 10s., the property of George Stevenson Rice, and also with steal- ing at the same time an overcoat, valued at 155., t4l property of John Meredith. The Chief Constable applied for a remand for a week, in order to inquire into the character of the prisoner. The evidence was very clear, and prisoner admitted the offence.—Thei remand was granted. TABLE, FLOOR AND HANGING LAMPS. New Designs now showing.—J, S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt. "The Sky Pilot." This was the title of an instructive yet humorous recital delivered by the Rev. J. Holden, of Bowdon to the members and friends of the Wycliffe Young People's Society in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall on Monday evening. The recital was highly entertaining throughout and much praise is due to the reciter for the able manner in which it was given.At the close of the recital Mr. G. Wallington said that they could not let Mr. Holden go without thanking him and he moved that the best thanks of the meeting be accorded to him for his excellent recital which they had all much enjoyed. Mr. J. W. Jeffery seconded and the motion was carried unanimously. Orford Old Boys' Be-Union. The members of the Orford Old Boys' Association held their fifth annual dinner in the schoolroom on Friday evening under the presidency of Mr. John Cann, the present schoolmaster. There was a good attendance, and an excellent menu had been prepared by the Atkinson's Cafe, Limited.- Immediately after dinner the annual businsss meeting of the Association was held. Mr. John Cann was unanimously re-elected president, Mr. Thomas Reddish secretary, and Coun- cillor Steel treasurer. The old committee was elected en bloc.— Business over, an excellent toast list was gone through.—Letters of apology were read from Messrs. H. P. Deasham, the Rev. W. Pollock-Hill, Mr. C. W. Davenport (an old boy) and others. During the evening songs were given by Messrs. T. Attwell, G. Bennett, F. Buckley, Mellor Ray, Ball and Gibson. The accompanist was Mr. W. Martin.—On the pro- position of Mr. F. H. Antrobus, seconded by Mr. W. Povey, a vote of thanks was accorded to the artistes or thir services. Buy HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASHLEY'S.—Advt. ‘COAGULINE,"BLINK,"TENASITINE.'--Cements for broken and other articles.—Advt. "The Wesley of Wales." This was the subject of a lantern lecture delivered under the auspices of the Bold-street Wesley Guild, delivered before a fairly large audience on Monday evening, by the Rev. W. A. Bryan Jones. In the absence of Mr. F. A. Harris the Rev. Thomas Miller presided. The lecturer gave a graphic description of the life of Mr. Howard Harris the great Welsh reformer. He commenced his work when but a boy when the land was steeped in vice. Harris and his followers were subjected to .great persecution, but Harris laboured on, and success accompanied his efforts wherever he went. When he started, the people of Wales were as ignorant of the Gospel as it was possible to be. He went through many trials and difficulties, but his work and perseverance were eventually rewarded. Harris made no attempt to preach ordinary sermons on particular texts for many years but he gave impromptu addresses "as the Spirit gave him utterance.' He never inflicted the same Isermon twice on anyone, and he preached the Gospel in all its simplicity and severity. He saw people rushing headlong to destruction, and _Fait forth every effort to reform them. The lantern slides were very fine. Several solos were rendered with effect by Miss Jones. On the proposition of the Chairman, seconded by Mr. Leech, assistant secretary, a vote of thanks was accorded to the lecturer, Miss Jones, Miss Weave? the pianist) and the lanternists. " HERALD " KITCHEN RANGES and "Peveril " Fire Grates are unsurpass74—lnspection invited. —J. S. GARLAND, 51, 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. —Advt.</p>
        <p>Mr. King on the House of Commons. Mr. A. J. King, M.P., gave a leeture at Wilmslow on "Some impressions of the House of Commons." Mr. King commenced by stating that the first impression he received was on the declaration of the poll at the election. He then proceeded to speak of the procedure observed at the assembling of Parliament, and then referring to the accommodation, he said he was struck by his smallness. Only 300 seats were provided, though the membership of the House approached 700. There was, however, sufficient room for those who were present on ordinary occasions. The House now met at 2.45 p.m. and sat till eleven .at night, no interval being allowed for dinner. On Fridays they met at twelve and finished at five. In the early after- noon and in the forenoon committees met, so obviously it would not be a satisfactory arrangement to have the House sttting earlier. The House- consisted of 670 members, and if a number of members rose to speak at once, the member whom the Speaker's eye caught was called upon, so it was hard for all desiring to speak to get a hearing. Tnis often caused all night sittings, which were unprofitable. To facilitate busi- ness four Grand Committees had been formed with ranch success. Dealing with members' duties not connected with the House Mr. King said the chief of these was the large hatch Of letters members received, the material of which was varied, coming from all classes. Buy HARRIS'S WILTSHIRE BACON at ASHLEY'S.—Advt. LINSEED COMPOUND,' Trade Mark of Kay's Compound Essence of Linseed for Coughs &amp; Colds.—Advt. " Memories of the Theatre." Mr. E. J. Hall delivered a lecture on this subject before the members of the Warrington Society on Monday evening. Mr. J. Longland presided over a good attendance..—Mr. E. J. Hall in an interesting way recalled his visits to theatres in London and the provinces, and gave impressions of the leading actors and actresses, including Henry Irving (whom he first saw in the "Lyons Mail " at the old Alexandra • Theatre Liverpool), Southern, Ellen Terry, William Ten iss, George Alexandra, Barry Sullivan, Mr. and Mrs. Kendal, Mrs. Patrick Campbell, Sir Charles Wyndham, Dan Leno, H. B. Irving, Beerbohm Tree, Lily Brayton and others. Describing his first visit to the Lyceum he said : the play was `Hamlet,"and the cast was a very powerful one. In addition to Irving and Ellen 'terry, William Terriss and George Alexander were In it, and I believe, a number of others whose names have become famous since then. It was wonderful. It quite goes without saying (but; all the same it would be very ungenerous not to say it), that we fell madly and head over ears in love with Ellenl Terry. It seems very absurd now, when one of us is, 'a grandfather, though A very young one, to think that if we were green and callow enough to lose our heads over an actress old enough than almost 'to be our mother, but—for excuse, if we need it I must say that we didn't know it, and she certainly didn't look it. She appeared to be "sweet seventeen" and divinely fair. The prettiness of her every moment, the way she caressed her throat with her dainty fingers —and, It was the very archest chin, That mockery ever ambushed in; the delightful voice, with the very, very slight huski- ness that was always peculiar to her; the eloquent and modest way in which we both for the first time saw and heard eyes and tongue confess love —why, what would you? as the French say. And, then There's rue for you, and here's some for me. There may have been greater actresses than Ellen Terry was in the prime and pride of her life, and I suppose there have been, but none have made a greater impression, or a sweeter, than she did on the great British public. When 1 first saw her, and for 20 years afterwards, she was charming. -On the motion of Mr. J. Rymer Young, seconded by Mr. Lofts house, and supported by the Rev. C. Harvey Cook,and Messrs. Beck, Jenkins and Madeley, a hearty vote of thanks was passed to Mr. Hall for his lecture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WOMAN'S WORRIES.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN'S WORRIES.</p>
        <p>Said a physician: "I wonder that women fail to appreciate how much nervous force as well as physical strength they consume in worrying over the little things of life. Look at the mother and housewife as she goes about her tasks, and on. serve how often she utters an impatient exclama- tion, how often she sighs over her servant's shortcomings, how often she starts nervously at a noise from one of the children. And each time that she loses control over herself, her nerves, her temper, she loses just a little nervous 'force, just a little physical • well-being, and moves a fraction of an inch further on in the path that leads to premature old age and to invalidism."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SECRET OF A HAPPY LIFE.</head>
        <p>THE SECRET OF A HAPPY LIFE.</p>
        <p>On the walls of an Old Ladies' Home hangs the photograph of a womanly face of rare sweet- ness, strength, and serenity. ' The key to this life is found in the following set of rules, taste- fully printed and enclosed in the same frame with the photograph: You sometimes see a woman whose old age is exquisite, as was the perfect bloom of her youth. She seems condensed sweetness and grace. You wonder how this has come about; you wonder how it is that her life has been a long and happy one. Here are some of the reasons:</p>
        <p>She knew how to forget disagreeable things. 'She understood the art of enjoyment. She kept her nerves well in hand, and inflicted them on no one.</p>
        <p>She believed in the goodness of her own daughters and in that of her neighbours. She cultivated a good digestion. She mastered the art of saying pleasant v ds. She did not expect too much from her fvends. She made whatever work came to her con- genfral. _ _ _</p>
        <p>- She retained her illusions, and did not believe that all the world was wicked and unkind. She relieved the miserable and sytnpathised with the sorrowful.</p>
        <p>She retained an even disposition and made the best of everything. She did whatever came to. her cheerfully and well.</p>
        <p>She never forgot that kind words and a smile cost nothing, but are priceless treasures to the discouraged.</p>
        <p>She did unto others as she would be done by; and now that old age has come to her and there is a halo of white hair about her head, she is loved and considered.</p>
        <p>This is the secret of a long life and a happy one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
