      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Lei, Saturday November 30, 1907.</head>
        <p>Lei, Saturday November 30, 1907.</p>
        <p>WAR OR PEACE.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON THE NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE DEBATE.</p>
        <p>A Correspondent writes -- - - -</p>
        <p>Those wh-o attended the National Service League debate on Monday last can scarcely have derived much satisfaction from reflection upon the proceedings. On neither side was there clear or convincing argument, and it must have impressed others besides myself that the pertinent points were allowed to pass almost unnoticed.</p>
        <p>If I may trespass upon your space I should like to refer to the very simple statement of the case made by Captain Crosfield. .An ambulance man, he said in effect, is trained to his work, but he does not go about tripping people in the street to secure an opportunity to show his skill. A fireman, because he is tiained to put out fires does not go about firing buildings; nor will those who are trained for war be likely to go about seeking to provoke it. That is a perfectly fair and straightforward state- ment and it puts the case in a nutshell. If there is an analogy between the work of the fireman, ambulance man and soldier, the National Service League has a claim upon our interest. But does the analogy exist ? The first two are trained to save life, but in no case to destroy it. There is no element of covetousness lying behind their labours, nor can they be ever used for purposes of national aggression or national theft. Can we say that of the soldier? It is a commonplace of history that trained men of war have been used in every generation to enforce injustices upon weaker nations and to forcibly appropriate their territory or goods. Will any sane man contend that there was any analogy between the part played by the soldier in the late South African war and the part played by the fireman at home?</p>
        <p>The analogy of the policeman was also pressed upon the meeting and we were also assured with great solemnity that those who object to soldiers have no right to bar their doors at night. The minister of religion who advanced this suggestion had surely not given himself time for consideration. The policeman exists to enforce the observance of ascertained laws which are laid down for individuals. Are there any such laws for nations? If thete is an international law which prohibits stealing, it will be a legitimate thing to bring force to bear upon the thief. The ideal state of things would be one in which there were careful international laws, with just soldiering enough to enforce those laws and no more. But does anyone suggest that we have got to that stage and that the soldier is simply the international policeman? Surely not!</p>
        <p>There is nothing in modern life which furnishes an analogy to the soldier and those who desire to be fair will not press analogies upon us. We take leave to judge war by its own appalling record and we refer to history as the readiest answer to those who pro- pound schemes for the making of soldiers who are guaranteed not to be dangerous to our peace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
