        <p>ladies voices in taking up an entry in the demon chorops but it was readily put right by an ever- watchful conductor. Penny Popular Concerts. The music for the principal voices was in the hands Another large audience assembled at the concert of Miss Gertrude Lonsdale, Mr. Alfred Heather and held in Bold-street schoolroom on Saturday evening. Mr. Fowler Burton, and, while one could scarcely wish The Rev. T. Miller presided, and during an interval for a more uniformly capable trio, Mr. Heather is spoke strongly upon tbe evils of gambling, and urged especially to be congratulated on 'the deep feeling all present to have nothing to do with it. An excel- shewn in his singing in the character of " Gerontius. lent programme had been arranged. Miss A. Wood- The orchestra was without doubt the finest ever heard land gave very tasteful interpretations of songs, and in Warrington on an occasion like that under notice. highly pleased the audience. Mr. Hughes sang several Numbering some 50 to 60 players every instrdment in songs in creditable style, and Messrs. Wild and Elgar's monumental score was, we believe,represented, Stewart scored a great success with their concertina and so amenable did they prove to the conductor's ; duets. Selections were also given by several members will in the all-essentials of tempo, tone-volume, of Mr. Brough's Juvenile Choir who fully maintained and marks of expressirm, as to contribute immensely their high reputation. Mr. H. Roberts was the to the signal and dignified success to which the society humorist. has so happily brought its opening concert of the season.</p>
        <p>The performance of "Gerontius " was preceded by a most excellent rendering of Schubert's Unfinished Symphony. 7</p>
        <p>CHRONIC ASTHMA with Hemorrhage of Lungs. Extraordinary Results from</p>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Beard, of 40, Hucknall-road, Carring- ton, Nottingham, writes :—" I suffered for live years from chronic asthma and shortnoes of breath, with periodical and dangerous attacks of bleeding of the lungs, doctors declared me incurable, and I gave up all hope of ever being better. "One day, as a last resource, I began to take Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and it at once checked the bleeding and eased the spasms, when doctors' medicine and all else bad failed. I was very bad when I begau taking Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, but now I am able to follow my employment, and I am sure your wonderful remedy has prolonged my life." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is the purcst, safest, and most reliable remedy extant for coughs and colds, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, sore throat, hoarseness, old-age coughs, children's conghs,and all chest and lung troubles. Price *I., is. 107 and 2s. 9d., of all chemists and drug storeti everywhere.</p>
        <p>ele Ned,—l hope you are enjoying good health by now. I was so sorry to hear you were ill, but delighted to hear that you were better when you wrote last. _I am send- ing you a short composition, which I should like to be printed. I think it was very nice of you to print the piece of poetry I sent last, "A Mortifying Mistake.'?—l now remain, with heaps of love, FLORENCE E. DIRK.</p>
        <p>THE FLOWER THAT FORGOT ITS NAME. Thousands of years ago God gave names to all the flowers. They were very proud of their names—the stately rose, the pure white lily, the dreamy violet, the April primrose, the straight slender tulip, the pansy with its laughing eyes. For amusement they repeated their new names to themselves, all but one small flower, that :bent beside a rivulet and wept. He was very, very tiny, but he seemed to have a great sorrow. "How now ?" asked the rose. "What is the matter ?" The little blossom wept silently. "Don't cry," continued tht rose. "Take pattern from the pansy yonder, and laugh. Why, even the shy little violet is more cheerful than you." "Alas," re- plied the flower, between its sobs, "I would like to laugh, but I am too unhappy. I have forgotten my name." "That is too bad," answered the rose. "We are sorry, but we cannot help you. God alone knows your name." And with this the rose and the other flowers went to their evening rest. Only the sad, small thing by the brooklet remained awake. God, in the depth of night, came to the weeping flower, and said, softly, "What is thy trouble I" "My name! my name! I have forgotten my name." "Dry thy tears," said God; "I will give you another name. You shall be called 'forget-me-not.'" This made the flower very happy, and it lifted its blue eyes in gratitude to Heaven.</p>
        <p>THE FOUNTAIN.</p>
        <p>Into the sunshine, Full of the light, Leaping and flashing From morn till night. Into the moonlight, Whiter than snow, Waving so flower-like oWhen the winds blow. Into the starlight, Rushing in spray, Happy at midnight, Happy at day. Full of all nature, Nothing can tame, Changed every moment, Ever the same. Ceaseless aspiring,</p>
        <p>Ceaseless content, Darkness or sunshine,</p>
        <p>Thy element. Glorious fountain!</p>
        <p>Let my heart be Fresh, changeful, constant, Upward, like thee! R. WARZTIRTON,</p>
        <p>THE CHERRIES,</p>
        <p>Sabina, the daughter of rich parents, had a little room for herself, but it was always un- tidy. All the good advice of her mother, that she should keep it in better order, was in vain. One Sunday afternoon she had just finished dressing herself, and was about to go out, when the daughter of a neighbour brought her a basket of fine black cherries. As tables and window-sill were already covered with clothes and other things, Sabina set the basket on a chair, which was covered over with blue silk stuff, and then went out with her mother for a walk. Late in the evening she came back to her room very tired, and threw herself on a chair. But she jumped quickly up again, with a cry of dismay; she had seated herself on the basket of cherries. Her white silk frock was stained with the juice of the ripe fruit. She was greatly vexed, anti all the more because she knew that if she had kept her room tidy, a 9 her mother wished, the cherries wouki have been on the table and not on the chair. DAISY STOCK'S.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l have such a beautiful pet dog. When I let him out in the morning he jumps up me and nearly knocks me down. Its colour is black and white. Sometimes we get a piece of string and put it in his mouth, and play at horses. If we throw a piece of bread he will catch it. If we ask him to kiss us he will lick us. When I go pulling plums, if there is one dropped, he will eat it.</p>
        <p>WILLIE RU SBY.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—My chief pet is a fantail pigeon. I had a pair at first, but one died in the winter time. When spring came I got a blue pigeon, and after a short time they mated, and began to lay eggs and rear young ones. They have reared four lots of young ones, and spoilt two They are all so tame that we have to be careful and watch the cats don't go near them, or else ,they would kill them. The white pigeon is so full of war that it will go into our neighbour's dovecote and peck the young ones on the back till they are dead. I am careful about keeping the cages nice and clean, because they do not like to roost in a dirty cage.' Now I must close with love.</p>
        <p>LESLIE PALLEY,</p>
        <p>John Bright says, that "Considering what it now costs a man to get into Parliament, M.P. must stand for money power."</p>
        <p>It may serve as a comfort to us in all our calamities and afflictions, that he that loses anything and gets wisdom by it, is a gainer by the loss.</p>
        <p>At a table-d'hilte in Normandy a very polite gentleman seizes the cider carafe and pours out for all his neighbours. "Madame, a little cider?" "Oh, monsieur, thank you!"" And you, monsieur:" "With pleasure. But you are giving me all; you have none left for your- self." The gentleman. with satisfaction: " Ah, now I can 'have some fresh!"</p>
