      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>MI DE.</head>
        <p>MI DE.</p>
        <p>I hope all of you read about my special Christmas competition in last week's paper. In case some of you missed it, I will tell you all about the prizes in my letter to-day:— First, then, I shall offer a ss. casket of Fry's Chocolate to the sender of the best article for a poor child. This may be any article of clothing. To the senders of the three next best I shall give three 2s. 6d. boxes of Fry's Choco- late. Also, as a first prize, a 2s. 6d. fancy box and two is. 6d. boxes of Fry's Chocolate to the best in the following Competitions:— For the best Short Christmas Story, about 200 or 300 words. For the best J6ke. For the best Conundrum, and for the best piece_ of _Poetry.</p>
        <p>To all those Who send any contribution, if they are not prize-winners, I shall give a little fancy mirror.</p>
        <p>All efforts must be sent me by the 14th of December.</p>
        <p>You will see by the above particulars every competitor, if le or she does not secure a prize, will receive a little pocket mirror. The "My Pet" letters have been a great suc- cess, and 1 must now ask you to turn your very best attention to the Christmas announce- ments.</p>
        <p>Welcome to Mona Scott, Neville Scott, Elsie Sloan, Dorothy Webber, Constance Underwood, Jessie Underwood, Beatrice Harris, Audrey Webb, Edith King, May Andrews, Alice Wright, Hilda Wright, Gladys Kilby, Dorothy Chesire, Hilda Bandy, Emily Allwood, William All- wood, Lily Kaye, Agnes Wiltshire, Myra Wilt- shire, Violet Wiltshire, Marion Farmer, Edith Deadlier, Florence Langley, Elvie Yates, Beatrice Yates, Grace Ward, Minnie Gromne, Emily Corley, Lilla Astling, Daisy Swain, Con- stance Warrillow, Marjorie Warrillow, May Tring, Gladys Sharpe, Lily Derbyshire. You see by the above that quite a lot of new members are joining our League. Well, I hope that all of them will send on contribu- tions.</p>
        <p>With love, from</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>THE STUDENT'S TRICK. A young collegian of the western world Was walking where a rippling streamlet purl'd. His worthy tutor (Heaven such tutors send! For he the title bore of "children's friend ") Was the companion of his lonely walk, And sought to improve his charge by serious</p>
        <p>talk; When, lo a pair of labourer's shoes they see Lying beneath the shadow of a tree. Seeing the owner's day's work nearly done, The college youth propos'd to have some fun; "Let's hide them," said the youth, "and watch</p>
        <p>to see _ "How great the man's perplexity will be." Not so," the•tutor, gravely smiling, said, A pleasant thought has come into my head, You're ;rich, young man, I would a lesson teach; Come, let us slip a dollar into each, And then, with pleasure true, without alloy, We'll watch to see the humble labourer's joy." The youth assented. Then with stealthy pace And noiseless step, they sought their hiding- place. And now approached toil's care-worn son, Warned by the fast declining sun; In putting on his stiffen'd boot, Some hard obstruction met his foot. Judge ye how great was his surprise, When a bright dollar met his eyes! Astonishment now fill'd his mind, He look'd before, he look'd behind, He look'd till he had strained his sight, Then pocketed the dollar bright; He stoop'd to fit the other shoe, When lo new wonders rise to view. With wonder and with love profound, He knelt upon the grassy ground, . And loudly thanked the God of Heaven For this his botlnty kindly given. And in his prayer he mention made Of ailing wife and children without bread, Whom some kind hand unknown had sav'd From sipking in a wretched grave. With tearful eye the student stood, He felt. the bliss of doing good, And said that he could now believe "More blessed 'tis to give than to receive." JOSEPH BREARLEY.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—We have already thought about making some clothes for the poor. We are sending you a few new members to join the Children's Corner.—Your loving nieces, CONNIE AND DORIS KNIGHT.</p>
        <p>Constance Underwood, Jessie Underwood, Beatrice Harris, Audrey Webb, Edith King, May Andrews, Alice Wright, Hilda Wright, Gladys Kilby, Dorothy Chesire, Hilda. Bandy, Emily Allwood, William Allwood, Lily Kaye, Agnes Wiltshire, Myra Wiltshire, Violet Wilt- shire, Marion Farmer, Edith Deamer, Florence Langley, Elvie Yates, Beatrice Yates, Grace Ward, Minnie Groome, Emily Corley, Lilla Astling, Daisy Swain, Constance Warrillow, Marjorie Warrillow, May Tring, Gladys Sharpe, Lily Derbyshire.</p>
        <p>Why should phy-sieians have a greater horror of the sea than anybody else ?—Because they are more liable to sea sickness.</p>
        <p>Surely if a medical quack can be called a woman because he's a Charlotte Anne, a chemist might be called a ditto„ as he is an Anne Eliza (analyser).</p>
        <p>"Landlady," said he, "this coffee isn't settled." "No," she replied, "but' it comes as near it as your last month's board bill does."</p>
        <p>Arithmetical problem.—lf a baker's wages are thirty shillings a week, what's the pay of a good loafer who has been bred to the profes- sion?"</p>
      </div>
