      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Broughton Rangers v. Warrington.</head>
        <p>Broughton Rangers v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men had an unpleasant experience at Broughton on Saturday, when they were not only beaten by 8 points to 3, but were obliged to endure the most miserable weather. Rain fell in torrents throughout nearly the whole of the match, the ground was like a quagmire, and the game finished in semi-darkness. It was really not fit for football, but League fixtures have to be filled when possible. Warrington were not at full strength, for Kenyon stood down at the last minute, and Jordan filled the vacancy. The Rangers had Wilson and James away. There were only about 2,500 spectators. Teams : Broughton Rangers: Barlow, back ; Handyman, Hogg, Barris, and Winskill, three-quarter backs; Horton and Flynn, half backs ; Clampitt, Grainy, Beetharu, Ruddick, Darlisrn, and Mottram, forwards. Warrington : Tilley, back; Jordan, Dickinson, Taylor and Brooks, three-quarter backs; Lees and Jenkins, half Lacks; Sbugars, P. Boardmau, A. Board- man, Belton, Harmer, and Thomas, forwards. Referee, Mr. E. U. Smirk, Preston. On commencing hostilities the advantage rested with the Rangers, and Flynn being in possession, the Warrington line was threatened. Harris, however, was baulked and preferred to take a drop at goal, which travelled outside. Premature tactics by the home backs got them penalized. Warrington made further piogress, and for a time the Rangers were subjected to a hot fusilate, Jenkins, Lees, Dickinson and Jordan each making futile attempts to break through. Return- ing once more to the Warrington end, Flyna served Harris' who dropped a goal. A passing mu betweun Lees, Dickinson, and Jordan ended in the wing man being forced into touch near the lig&amp; and, from the succeeding pack the West Lancashire men had hard luck, for Lees dribbled over, but had the mortification of seeing Hardyman just kick dead as the Warrington skipper was in the act of grounding. Tilley displayed magnificent defence, and at half-time the Broughton men led by two points to nil. The players immediately crossed over, and Warrington were early on the attack. From a pack near the line Jenkins passed to Lees, who, in making his effort to cross, allowed the leather to slip from his hold and Warrington's chance to take the lead was thus baffled. The locals again became per- sistent on the attack and a daring save by Tilley was duly applauded. A mistake by Hogg and Winskill should most certainly have been turned to good account by tbe visitors, and only the holding nature of the ground could be offered as an excuse. Hogg get the ball at his toes at centre, steered the ball over the Warrington line,and was awarded a try. Barlow failed at goal. Immedintrly ' after the re-start the Broughton men became aggressive and despite a good tackle of Tilley, Harris managed to get clear and 1 place the Rangers further ahead. The rain had now ceased and Warrington improved wonderfully in the last few minutes. Taylor and Lees exchanged, and no opening was made for Brookes who missed the wet ball with a clear opening. Jordan next broke away, and escaped the vigilance of Hardyrnan, but the superior speed of the ex-Leicester player saved the Rangers. Warripgton continued to play a superior game and the forwards rushed to the line, where Lees crossed, but Thomas failed at goal. After this success the game took an even turn and time came as a relief to spectators and players alike. Result : Broubton Rangers, one goal two tries (eight points) ; Warrington one try (3 points):</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
