      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>MONTH OF METEORS.</head>
        <p>A MONTH OF METEORS.</p>
        <p>November is a month of meteors, and dur- ing its course five dates are marked by the occurrence of meteoric showers. These are 2nd, 14-16th, 20-23rd, 23-24th, and 25th. The best known of these showers are the Leonids (14-16th), and the Andromidids (23- 24th). And these two are periodical, that is to say, specially bright displays are looked for at definite intervals. Every thirty-three years a specially large display of Leonids is expected by the astronomer, while the Andro- midids make their more brilliant show every six years. The next bright period for the former is 1933, and for the latter 1912-13.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>NOTABLE METEORITE COLLECTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTABLE METEORITE COLLECTIONS.</p>
        <p>the ground it bec-olner- Jailing star reacho. or metallic fragment. It is eagerly colleitki,- and in earlier days divine honours were paid it. The oldest of the great collections in the world is said to be that in the museum at Vienna. During the course of 100 years 2,075 specimens have been gathered together, re- presenting falls from 615 different localities. The total weight of these is al tons. In the Museum of Natural History, New York, again, there is a collection representing 603 meteoric falls. It is known as the Ward- Coolney collection, and was brought together in the space of ten years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>NEW THEORY OF MATTER.</head>
        <p>THE NEW THEORY OF MATTER.</p>
        <p>The new view as to the constitution of matter set forth by Professor J. J. Thomson in his book, "The Corpuscular Theory of Matter," may be said to have originated in the brilliant researches of Sir • William Crookes. The green phosphorescence which illuminates the sides of a vacuum tube when a current of electricity is sent through it has been shewn to be caused by negatively-elec- trified particles shot off from the electrode where the negative electricity enters the tube. This is proved, says the Globe, by the fact that they are deflected by the magnet,. just as negatively - electrified particles of matter would be. Again, If the emissions from the electrode are caught in a metal vessel this is found to receive a charge of negative elec- tricity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>CORPUSCLES THE BASIS OF MATTER.</head>
        <p>CORPUSCLES THE BASIS OF MATTER.</p>
        <p>- - Such corpuscles, or negatively-electrified particles, form the basis of the new theory of matter, and must take the place of the old atom in our conception of its constitu- tion. And whether such corpuscles are found producing the phosphorescence in a Crookes' tube, in the emanations of radium, or escaping from the surface of a metal, they are of the same nature, viz., negatively- electrified particles. In size, again, they are much smaller than atoms. Hence it is sug- gested that they are constituents of all atoms, and finally arises the theory that the atoms of ordinary matter are made up of such corpuscles, or electrons, as they have .been named. Electrons are often spoken of as negatively-electrified particles, but they are also sometimes looked upon as particles of neptive electricity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>ELECTRICITY IS ATOMIC.</head>
        <p>ELECTRICITY IS ATOMIC.</p>
        <p>These electrons being the smallest possible particles of electricity may be called atoms of electricity in the original meaning of the word. Thus the structure of electricity is atomic, and the new theory of matter is briefly that it. is made up of atoms of elec- tricity. Evelry atom of matter, in the old mehning of the words, then, is made up of a number of atoms of negative electricity. And the varying properties of the different elements are due to the varying number of atoms of electricity in their atoms. The elements of least atomic weight will contain the fewest electrons, and those with higher atomic weight the greater number. Hydro- gen, the element of least atomic weight, has been estimated to contain 600 electrons in its atom. All other known elements will contain more in proportion to their atomic weight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>CURIOUS CAUSE OF TREE TWISTS.</head>
        <p>CURIOUS CAUSE OF TREE TWISTS.</p>
        <p>Curious twists are observed in many tree trunks, and inquiry suggests the surprising conclusion that they are produced by the earth's rotation, like the twists of storms and the whirls seen in water. M. Vanden Broeck, the Belgian geologist, points out that if con- ditions of growth were the eau the twist- ing should follow the sun's apparent path. In at least 990 out of 1,000 trees the reverse is true, and it may be that the twist is usually to the left in the northern hemi- sphere and to the right—or with the clock— in the southern hemisphere, like the turn of the cyclonic storms and water vortices. This difference is due to the earth's rotation. M. Jepn Brunhes notes that it was shewn some years agq..that the wiuds Oue,seto .the .earth'a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <p>;notion mow steaalty at a season nvnela vege- tation is active and sensitive, and a slight continuous bending and turning then would be likely to affect the tree permanently.</p>
        <p>HOW OUR SOLAR SYSTEMS AROSE. Professor Lowelh of Flagstaff, Arizona, is</p>
        <p>perhaps the greatest living authority on the planet Mars, and in the Century Magazine he has been discoursing on "Mars as the Abode of Life." He tells of the catastrophic origin of the universe of which We form part, and this is how he recounts'the tragedy: The epic of our solar system began with a great catastrophe. "Two suns met. Fatal to what had been, the eient dated a stupendous cosmic birth.</p>
        <p>It is more than likely that one or both of the colliding masses were dark bodies, dead suns, such as now circle unseen in space amid the bright ones we call the-stars. Pro- bably this is, for the same reason that the men who have been far outnumber the men who are. It is not to be supposed that the two rovers actually struck, the chances being against so head-on an encounter; but the effect was as disastrous. Tides •:saised in each by the approach</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>TORE BOTH TO FRAGMENTS,.</head>
        <p>TORE BOTH TO FRAGMENTS,.</p>
        <p>the ruptured visitant passing on and leaving a dismembered body behind in lieu of what had been the other. That the stranger con- tinued on its way is shewn by the present moment of momentum of our system. For it is very small, and the fact can be proved to mean that after the encounter its matter still lay massed for the most part in a single centre. Thus, what had been one sun was left alone, with its wreckage strewn about it. Mas.ses large and small made its outlying fragments, scattered through space in its vicinity, while a• shattered nucleus did it for core. So much of its history" we learn from the tiniest of its constituents now, the meteorites. To meteorites thus attaches a peculiar importance; for they are Rosetta stones for the decipherment of what went before. These fragments formed the solar system of the sun and the planets. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>LIFE HISTORY OF A PLANET</head>
        <p>LIFE HISTORY OF A PLANET</p>
        <p>was long or short in proportion to its size. If little, it ran through its gamut of change fast, and that gamut was brief; if large, it tarried in its several stages, and those stages were themselves drawn out. Six stages may conveniently be distin- guished in the progress of a planet from sun to cinder, all of which, will be traversed by the body, if it be sufficiently big. If it be of asteroidal size, it virtually knows none of them, remaining meteoric from first to last. The six periods may be designated: 1. The Sun Stage. Hot enough to .emit light.</p>
        <p>2. The Molten gtage. Hot, —but lightles 3. The Solidifyiiit Stage. Solid surface formed. Ocean basins determined. Age of metamorphic rocks. 4. The Terraqueous Stage. Age of sedimen- tary rocks. 5. The Terrestrial Stage. Oceans have dis- appeared. 6. The Dead Stage. Air has departed. In the second stage aro found Neptune, Uranus, Saturn, and Jupiter; in stage four the Earth; in stage five Mars; and in stage six the Moon. The seas have disappeared from Mars, and, in addition, owing to the slight shrinking of the planet's crust, thanks to its small size and low heat, there are ne mountains on it.</p>
        <p>Life of Huxley.</p>
      </div>
