      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>CONGO ATROCITIES.</head>
        <p>CONGO ATROCITIES.</p>
        <p>PROTEST MEETING IN THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>Letter from Mr. A. If. Crosfield, M.P.</p>
        <p>ROMAN CATHOLIC OPPOSITION.</p>
        <p>Lively Scenes.</p>
        <p>A meeting promoted by tho Congo Reform Association to protest against the atrocities on the Congo was held in the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening There was a good attendance, Mr. C. E. Parker presiding. The principal speakers were the Right Hon. Lord Monkswell, and the Rev. J. H. Harris (late Congo-801010 Missionary) and others present on the platform were : The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), the Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis), Revs. W. Brace camp, W. Pollock-Hill, G. Vale-Owen, A. M. Mitchell, H. B. Firth, J. Yonge, J. S. Hughes, H. Wall, W. Mellor, F. Vaughan, C. Harvey-Cook, J. F. Matthews (secretary), Alderman Bennett, Councillor Stott, Captain Crosfield, and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. Before the meeting began, lantern views of scenes in the atrocity-ridden district were exhibited. LETTERS OF APOLOGY.</p>
        <p>-- - The Rev. J. F. M.trrnEws said Canon Stevenson was unable to be present but wished to express his entire sympathy with the object of the gathering. Letters had also been received from the Revs. Sidney Pitt, J. Buglass and T. Miller, and Alderman Ellison. He (Mr. Matthews) wished to say how very cordially the clergy of the Anglican Church had co-ooperated to make the meeting possible. - MR. CROSFIELD'S OPINION. The DUMAN had received the following letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P.: Hyde Park Hotel, London, S.W. November 27th, 1907.</p>
        <p>My dear Parker, May I send a message of good wishes for your meeting this evening, called to deal with the Congo question. We have here one of those issues which stand outside the ordinary lines of parties and sects, so that Tories and Radicals, Nonconformists, Agnostics, Churchmen, Catholics, may all sink their differences in working for a common and a humane cause. Tnere has been a good deal of special plead- ing by a few very much interested people in Belgium who have tried to manufacture public opinion over here in their favour in the modern style. But among all those who best know the facts, including Church- men like the Archbishop of Canterbury and the Bisoop of Southwark, and Nonconformists like Dr. Clifford, there is a consensus of opinion—and a very strong opinion it is. We know that the Congo Free State was created with the consent of Great Britain for the purpose of improving the condition of the natives, and securing fair treatment for all traders alike. Under the present regime neither of these conditions has been fulfilled. The main fact of the situation is,l take it,just this, that the Rubber monopoly has been worked so as to produce a maximum of immediate profit at the cost of appalling suffering and loss of life. It is also notorious that foreign trade of a legitimate description is hampered in every possible way. In emphasising these facts we do not bring any charges against the Belgian people who have been in no way responsible for the terrible amount of cruelty and the fiendish atrocities that have been perpetrated on the Congo. The charge and the indictment are against a few unscrupulous and ruthless men. I have a very great respect for Sir Edward Grey and am far from criticising his diplomacy. On the contrary what we want to do is surely to strengthen his hands with the force of public opinion in regard to any steps which he may take for the reform of native administration and the maintenance of British rights While it is now suggested that the Congo Free State ought to be annexed by Belgium, the Bill for this purpose is in danger of being amended at King Leopold's Court 'in directions which would explicitly contravene Sir Edward Grey's declarations to the House of Commons. lam not behind the scenea and cannot estimate the special difficulties Sir Edward may have to contend with and I will not presume therefore to suggest the precise course which British diplomacy ought to take, but I feel sure that if Sir Edward takes ... . . cannot honestly say I regret the particular circum- stance which prevents me attending to-day's meeting! I can assure you that nothing less than that circum- stance would have sufficed to prevent me coming to the meeting to show one's sympathy with the cause and one's respect for our distiuguished guest on the</p>
        <p>occasion. Trusting your meeting will have great significance and effect. Believe me, very faithfully yours, A. 11. CROSFIELD. THE CHAIRMAN'S ADDRESS.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said: Wears met to-night seriously to consider a great human tragedy, a tragedy which in its cruelty cannot be surpassed, if indeed it can be equalled by the wrong perpetrated by man upon his fellows in the blackest pages of the records of our race. The story is not one that has been evolved out of the brains as some consider of warm hearted and sentimental missionaries who see things through other-world glasses but is based upon well authenticated facts which prove beyond question that the rule of Leopold of Belgium of the part of Africa of which he was made trustee by thd powers of Europe twenty-two years ago is in its results the most terrible that ever stained the history of the human race. (Applause.) I do not intend to dwell upon the details. I can best serve the purpose of our meeting if I refer you to what men in positions of authority and responsibility have said and acknow- ledge(' in regard to this question. These will illustrate to you that those in the best positions to judge have accepted the facts as incontrovertible and directed their thoughts towards a remedy. The Archbishop of Canterbury in the debate in the House of Lords last July said : "When we look into the facts, it seems to me that the mischief which finds its worst development in the horrible outrages is really fundamental in the manner in which the government of the State is carried on. The mutilations, the cruelties the "Hostage" system—these are simply appalling. But we mislead both ourselves and other people if we dwell too exclusively upon them. Those are the natural and inevitable outcome of a system which is fundamentally bad, and which is contradictory to the very principles laid down in the Berlin Convention twenty years ago. The land is governed or utilised, now, not for the good of its inhabitants—that is, not for the reasons for which the State was allowed to come into existence—but for the gain or profit of its so-called owners in Europe. The outrages done by the native soldiem are simply due to the necessities imposed upon them by their European masters." This is an important point which I wish to urge upon this meeting at its outset. We have to realize that no tinkering with the details upon the spot will avail anything, this system which produces these details must be entirely swept away. (Applause.)As long as you have an absentee Sovereign who uses his powers not to protect his people but for trading purposes in which the lust for gold becomes the paramount motive.so long will we have the rights of the natives abused and their limbs and lives sacrificed to satisfy this lust. As the Archbishop truly says: "These are the natural and inevit- able outcome of a system which is fundamentally bad.' But the question is not one concerning the Congo basin alone ; it is one affecting the whole of the native' races in South Africa. Sir Harry Johnston, in a memorable letter, has written :—" Unless some stop can be put to the misgovernment of the Congo region I venture to warn those who are interested in African politics that a movement is already begumand is spread- ing fast, which will unite the negros against the white race, a movement which prematurely stamp out the beginnings of the new civilization we are trying to implant, and against which movement, except so far as the actual coast line is concerned, the resources of men and money, which Enrope can put into the field, will , be powerless." From every point of view then whether we look at the question from a religious standpoint from which we view the matter in the light of the great fundamental fact of the Christian faith that all men are brothers and united by the great self-sacrifice of the elder brother Jesus Christ our Lord ; or whether we approach it from a humanitarian point of view only and consider the spread of civilization and the elementary rights of humanity; or whether we look at. the ques- tion from a national point of view and consider the honour and the security of the British Empire—from whichever aspect we view this question we are forced to the conclusion that this system must be brought to an end, and that very speedily. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>LORD MONKWELL'S STRONG WORDS.</head>
        <p>LORD MONKWELL'S STRONG WORDS.</p>
        <p>Lord MONKSWELL said they were met not as belong- ing to any particular creed or party, but as men and women who were determined to show that they bad all the old English hatred of inhumanity and injustice. (Applause.) England, he was glad to think, was at last giving forth on this. question no uncertain sound. They had long been trying to teach the people of England what the issue was and they had sometimes been bitterly disappointed at the reception of their observations. They bad found themselves face to face , with the densest ignorance on the question and he thought that ignorance wns not entirely dispelled or</p>
        <p>.„ For Bronchial Coughs take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/11, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
        <p>they would see an uprising even greater than they were now witnessing. The Congo system of Government was an organized system of robbery, torture and murder. It was fully shown that there had been no improvement up to that moment. England had an enormous responsibility in the matter—a greater responsi- bility than any of the other signatory powers of the Berlin Act which handed over the Congo to King Leopold. We made a grim and ghastly mistake 1 twenty-two years ago and we ought to rectify it—we 1 were bound in honour to rectify it—at the earliest 1 possible moment. We made the mistake with the best intentions, but they knew- where good intentions led to. (Laughter.) Good intentions in this case had led to the Congo hell. Lord Monkswell dealt with some of the apologies made for King Leopold. Continuing be said they had deliberately stayed their hands in the matter to find out what Belgium was going to do and whether there was anything in the plea that Belgium was going to annex the Congo. They entirely denied that King Leopold had the right to hand over the Congo to anybody except subject to the terms and restrictions imposed upon him, but not enforced, under the Berlin Act. A nominal annexation by Belgium that would leave King Leopold practically in absolute possession would be a mere mockery and something to which we could never set our hands. (Applause.) There was absolutely no provision made at present to safeguard any of the interests of the natives, supposing that Belguin did annex. "It is perfectly intolerable ' continued his lordship "that King Leopold, whose crimes are as colossal as his vices are mean, odious and contemptible, from whose nature no pitiful meanness is alien, who has cheated his own subjects and given to his illegitimate son the gold that he has coined from the Congo natives and which he promised to Belguim, surely it is intolerable that such a man should be allowed to continue to dispose of the lives and liberties of 15 millions of people as the trustee of Europe and the champion of - civilization and Christianity." (Applause.) The groat question now was supposing we were unable to arouse the conscience of Europe and other people would not do their duty, should we do our's single-handed, whatever happened ? (Applause.) The Prime Minister in a recent speech contrasted the hopes we had of King Leopold when we gave him the Congo with his performances, and said that the present position was absolutely intolerable and a menace to civilization. He added that the Government was deeply impressed with the sense of England's respon- sibility. Now, he (Lord Nlonkswell) took it that if those words meant anything they me int that in the last resort England would and must act alone. (Applause.) Sometimes the boldest course was the best course to adopt. (Applause.) REV. J. H. HARRIS'S EXPERIENCES. The Rev. J. H. HARRIS said that if there could be a pleasant feature connected with the campaign for the liberation of the natives of the Congo, it was the remarkable unanimity with which the subject was being faced by the people of this country. He did not think anyone could contradict him when he said that there had probably never been before the people of Great Britain a serious matter upon which all parties and denominations, all sections of public opinion were so absolutely united as they were on the Congo question. (Applause, and a VOICE: "Question.") He found on the platforms as he went up and down the country men drawn from all denominations. He had spoken with almost all the Bishops, with all the leading Nonconformists' with leading members of the Roman ( atholic Church and very often with fathers of that body, and over and above that the whole Press was united in condemnation of this awful iniquity and in emphasising Great Britain's responsibility. Mr. Harris repudiated the idea that they were attacking the , Belgian nation or the Belgian Government. Belgium 1 was no more responsible for the administration of the Congo than were Great Britain, France and Germany— indeed they might safely say that the responsibility for what was going on lay more upon the British people , than upon the Belgians, for the reason that the people 1 of England for years had been acquainted with what was 1 going on and had done nothing. l'hey were attacking the system that had been introduced into the Congo Valley by King Leopold,—introduced not merely in his capacity as Sovereign of the Congo but as trustee for Europe. To-day when we were faced with this colossal crime, this slave trade and atrocity, the people of England were not in a mood to surrender the responsibility they took upon them- selves on behalf of the natives twenty-three years ago. (Applause.) Even if there were no atrocities connected with the Congo regime, it would still be our duty to , struggle for the liberty of those people, for when specula- tors declared as their own personal property for their own ends the whole of the labour of a subject race, it was slavery pure aud simple. (Applause.) The case for reform did not rest upon the credibility of any number of witnesses for when they saw the system at work in the Congo any intelligent man or woman would know that the most inhuman barbarities were the order of the day. (Applause.) It was no use endeavouring to repri- mand a bite men or punish black men for the atrocities. The only way was to take the system root and branch from the Congo and give back to the natives the land and the liberty which was theirs. (Applause and a Voice : "You ought to do that with the Irish nation.") Mr. Harris said he was prepared to answer any questions at the close of his address, and he thought that he was was makin a. n.k .uppwaLar ea.e. rianirl--Litohr-Zteidri 6-4 cl the methods of torture adopted, and gave instances from his own personal observation of atrocities perpetrated. He gave the names of some natives he had known to be multilated and also produced photographs. The British Consul at Boma had said that King Leopold had directly and indirectly reduced the population of the Congo by at least three millions of human beings. Millions were being done to death in the interests of a miserable coterie in Brussels and the civilisation that pledged itself in the name of God to watch over those people sat still and looked calmy on. Oh people of England "continued Mr. Harris " where, oh where is your tradition, that you should sit by and see this thine' done in broad daylight and not rise in your thousands to insist that King Leopold shall take his hands off the throats of those helpless people." (Applause.) He hoped and believed the British Government would 1 shortly see its way clear to declare that we could only ; consent to Belgian annexation on the lines of the General Act of Berlin. He believed that if Sir Edward Grey were to put his foot down and say " The Congo inquity shall cease" the people of England would be I behind him to a man. (Applause.) QUESTIONS. Councillor TOOLE asked if Mr. Harris had the last 1 report of the British Consul?</p>
        <p>MT. HARRIS : Yes. Councillor TOOLE : Will you read it? Mr. HARRIS : I am afraid the last report is too long to read. I oan give an extract or two from it. Consul Mitchell, who has had 29 years' experience in Africa, says "In all my experience I have never seen such a miserable lot as these Congo people." In another part he says: "The natives are all disheartened and are unanimously of the opinion that they were better off under the Arabs."</p>
      </div>
