      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 30, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 30, 1907.</p>
        <p>tion of his superlative qualities as the ruler of his people. (Applause.) He (\lr. Yonge) was by no means a Republican. He was a loyal subject of the British Crown but he thought he was discerning enough to read that the present day signs pointed to the truth that the doctrine of the divine right of Kings was out of date and if Kings were to retain their sovereignties they must secure the lives of their people and obey the will of their people and do nothing which tampered with human freedom and safety. King Leopold had been sowing the wind and he was going Ito reap the whirl wind. There was a Judge in heaven and as surely as that was so, this cruel, tyrannicil and murderous King would come to his reward. King Leopold to-day was being far less the King of the Belgians than he was the King of the sweaters. (Applause.) ROMAN CATHOLIC OPPOSITION.</p>
