      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>May—Sloane.</head>
        <p>May—Sloane.</p>
        <p>The pretty village church of AR Saints, Daresbury, was the scene of a wedding which aroused great interest on Wednesday, when Miss Rita Sloane, daughter of Mr. and Mrs. William Sloane, The Beeches, Moore, was married to the Rev. Ernest EL Bedford May, chaplain of Christ Church College, Oxford, son of the Rev. E. H.May, of Bartlow Rectory, Cambridge. The bride and bridegroom are both well-known and esteemed in the district, Mr. May having at one time been curate of All Saints, Dares- bury. The wedding was an exceedingly pretty one and a large congregation assembled to witness the ceremony. The officiating clergy were the Rev.E.Fl.May father of the bridegroom, and the Rev.T.T.Lee- Jones, vicar of Dareibury. The service was fully choral. Mr. Frank Booth presided at the organ and while the guests were assembling played the Following selections : " Liebslied," Henselt ; " Grand Chceur," Salome ; "Festal March," Elvey ; "Rosainonde," Schubert. The bridal party was met at the church do)r by the clergy and choir who preceded them up the aisle, while the hymn " Thine for ever" was sung. The Psalm was I chanted to Barnby in E flat. At the close of the I service Mr. Booth played the " Bridal March " from " Lohengrin " and Mendelssohn's " Wedding I March:' The bride, who was given away by her I father, was beautifully attired in an ivory satin mousseline gown, with a rich silver girdle. The corsage was trimmed with satin roses and leaves i veined with silver thread and finished with ivory I mousseline de soie. She wore an embroidered tulle veil with orange blossom, heather and myrtle, and carried a lovely bouquet of white lilies. There were three bridesmaids —Miss Constance Sloane and Miss Winifred Sloane (sisters of the bride) and Miss Patricia Wyatt (niece of the bride). Miss Constance Sloane wore a handsome gown of cream net over chiffon and silk trimmed with fine lace and cloth of gold, with an Oxford blue hat and plumes. Misses Winifred Sloane and Patricia Wyatt were prettily attired lin ivory satin in quaint old English style. Miss Sloane wore an Oxford blue bonnet with chiffon strings, and Miss Wyatt an Irish cr )chet bonnet, the effect being very pleasing. The bridesmiids' bouquets were com- posed of chrysanthemums. The bride's mother was gowned in black mausseline-de-soie over white, the bodice being trimmed with gold and chenille. She wore a black hat with large ostrich plumes and carried a bouquet of Maluaaison carnations and lilies of the valey. Mr. H. A. Leeke, of Cambridge, discharged the duties of best man. After the ceremony a reception was held at the Beeches, 150 guests attending. The wedding cake, supplied by Messrs. Bolland,of Chester, was surmounted with satin flags bearing the Arms of Oxford and of the bridegroom's old college - Oriel. Later the happy couple, having received the heartiest congratulations of their friends, left for a honeymoon tour in the South of England. The bride's travelling dress was a brown costume with hat to match. LIST OF PRESENTS. The following is a list of the presents : Bridegroom to bride, diamond and ruby ring, gold bracelet, and diamond and sapphire ring Bride to bridegroom, suit case Rev. E. H. May, silver candlesticks, silver salver, coffee and tea pots Mrs. May, amethyst, topaz and ruby brooch, and silver tea caddy Mr. W. Sloane, cheque Mr. Sloane Walker, silver salver Mr. Charles NValker, silver flower vase Mesdames Jordan and Lockwood, silver cream jug Rev. and Mrs. L. J. T. Darwall, silver sugar basin Mrs. Robinson, brass spirit kettle Mrs. Wyatt, silver tea knives Sir Lees Knowles, silver salver Mr. Farrimond, silver salver Maids and men at Baeches, silver cream jug, sugar basin and salver Mrs. Pearson, silver cream jag Mr. Osmond Gallaher, silver vegetable dish Mr. and Mrs. Byrom Franceys, rose bowl and dish Mr. and Mrs. Yates-Baxter, silver dish Mr. W. Milner, frame Miss Okell, silver vase Mr. and Mrs. Forrest, silver teapot - Mr. and Miss Forrest, silver toast rack Mr. Seeker, silver flower stand The Rev. E. J. S. Barnes, bookcase Dr. and Mrs. E. Fox, lace handkerchiefs Mr. A. J. and Mr. W. H. Harris, dressing case Miss Dutton, Worcester vase Mr. and Mrs. Armitage. copper flower pot Mr. and Mrs. Herbert Woods, silver salt cellars Mr. and Mrs. John Fe.irclough, travelling clock Mr. and Mrs. E. Fairclough, silver cheese dish Moore Sunday School, silver ink pot Mrs. Ivor Jones, sugar sifter Mr. Alfred Read, four silver vases Mrs. NV. Cartwright, oak table Mrs. Cathcart Smith, butter dishes Miss E. Litton, oak knife box Mr. Sale, silver calendar Mrs. Middlebrook, silver hair brush Mrs. James Walker, silver dishes The Rev. G. Watkins' " Sluggard's Joy " Mrs. Harriet May, silver watch stand Miss May, silver fruit knives and forks Mr. G. H. Ridgwly, silver fish knives and forks Miss Gornall, sofa back Mrs. Segar Owen, china bowl Mr. and Mrs. E. Ridgway, silver mustard pot Mr. and Mrs. Wilfrid Blackly, calendar in frame Baroness Malortie, ruby ring Mr. Dyotc Darwall, silver clock Miss Sloane, picture Mr. Hale Thompson, cushion Mr. Lafontein, two silver vegetable dishes Mr. ani Mrs. Evans, silver sweet dish Canon and Mrs. Hopkins, silver gilt biscuit box Miss Amy May, barometer Rev. V. Doherty, letter case Rev. G. Sutherlend, coffee cups Mr. and Mrs. Pally, fish carvers Mrs. Robinson, thistle liquer glasses Mr. and Mrs. Peyton, silver bowl Mr. and Mrs. Brocklebank, silver pepper pots ( Mr. and Mrs.. Gascoyne, silver merrythought Miss Florence May, silver fruit spoons Mrs. Leigh, clock Rev. E. Smith, water colour The Parnich Parishioners, silver candlesticks Miss Milly Strond, silver clock Mr. Robinson, egg boiler Mr. John Huges, silver centrepiece Mrs. R. I. Wynne-Jones, tea set Mr. Francis Woods, silver-mounted bottle Mrs. Kay, cushion cover Mr. and Mrs. Quirk, silver tea service and salver Mr. Byrne, silver fruit stand Mr. and Mrs. Lester, silver flower vase Dr. and Mrs. Bower, silver sugar sifter Miss Johnson, silver muffin dish Rev. E. C. E. and Mrs. Carleton, silver frame . Mr. and Mrs. J. L. Jones,lsilvar flower vases Rev. Edmund May, silver sugar basin Miss Esdaile, two filigree tongs Rev. W. Milne Esdaile, lamps Mrs. Mundy, cheque Servants at Bartloav, silver sugar bas)a Canon Sanday book Captain Bridge, two pairs carvers Mr. Stainer, silver tankard Canon Bedford, silver bowl Mrs. Stead, cushion Mr. Haswell, silver teaspoons and tongs Rev. and Mrs. T. T. Lee-Jones, travelling clock Miss Elsie Lee-Jones, pair silver flower vases Mr. and Mrs. White, lace and pearl fan Captain and Mrs. Leuis-Wyatt, two pairs silver flower</p>
        <p>vases Rev. H. Clifton Smith, pearl brooch Mr. and Mrs. Frith, George IL silver cream lug Mrs rodd, silver jewel casket Miss Andrews; silver photo frame Miss Milner, pair silver candlesticks Miss Forrest, silver photo frame Mr. and Mrs. Whitley, silver bowl Mr. and Mrs. Houghton, carved oak table Mr. and Mrs. Williams, silver cake basket Dr. John Roberts, silver mounted coffee cups and silvar- butter dishes.</p>
