      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>MR. H. P. BRIERLEY'S SUICIDE. All Cash Deficiencies to be Made Good.</head>
        <p>MR. H. P. BRIERLEY'S SUICIDE. All Cash Deficiencies to be Made Good.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance Committee on November 25th, the following letter from Messrs. H. B. White and Sons was read :</p>
        <p>3, Egypt-street, Warrington, 25th November, 1907.</p>
        <p>Gentlemeu.</p>
        <p>We are requested by the widow and relatives of the late Mr. I. P. Brierley to inform you that it is their wish that no part of any loss which may have arisen from the late Mr. H. P.Brierley's acts or defaults shall fall upon the Ratepayers of the Borough of Warrington and they authorise us to state that they are prepared to pay in full all claims which can be shown to us to be justly due. _</p>
        <p>They also wish us to say that they much appreciate the kindness and sympathy which has been shown to them by Members of the Council and by the officials. Yours faithfully, _ H. B. WHITE AND SONS.</p>
        <p>Resolved, that the committee express their .great satisfaction with the conduct of the relatives in the matter and that the Town Clerk, Borough Surveyor and Borough Treasurer be authorised to meet and settle the amount with Mr. Aubrey White. The Health Committee has resolved, that a horse. trap and harness be purchased from the executors of the late IL P. Brierley for the use of this department, at the price of £6O.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>DR. FLITCROFT'S POSITION.</head>
        <p>DR. FLITCROFT'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>Resignation or Local Government Board Inquiry. LIVELY DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>AN AM EN D M ENT.</p>
        <p>At this stage the Rev. FaTnEn. WILSON, who was sitting in the body of the hall, rose and said : T have asked the Chairman to allow me to address the meeting but I am sorry to say he has refused to do so. (" Shame.") Mr. H. RIGBY asked to be allowed to move an amend- ment. The CHAIRMAN thought the purpose would be served by voting against the resolution. Mr. RIGBY : ff I vote against the resolution I may be voting against something that I agree with, but if you allow me to move an amendment it will make our position clear. The CHAIRMAN eventually decided to allow the amendment, and also, if the meeting wished it, to give Father Wilson an opportunity of speaking, as he was I sure he would take a fair and just view of the question. (Applause.) Mr. RIGBY then moved : "That in view of the ! British Consuls' last report which contradicts almost I all that has been said here to-night, this meeting asks ! for the dismissal of those Consuls. If they are not dismissed, then we must conclude that their report is true, and what we have heard to-night is untrue." Father WiLsoN rose to second the amendment and readily availed himself of the Rev. J. F. Matthews' sug- gestion that he should speak from the platform. The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS : I hope whenever I want to move a resolution at a Catholic meeting I shall receive the same courtesy. (Applause). The Rev. Father WILso.); said the Prime Minister in I his recent speech said: "Any attempt on the part of an outside power to influence the decision of the Belgians would naturally and very properly be resented." It was because he did not wish that we should incur the resentment of a free Sovereign and friendly Power in a rash and ill-considered manner that he asked the privi- lege of addressing the meeting. To justify them in adopting the resolution, which at least conveyed a very serious indictment against a friendly Power, it should have been proved that the administration of the Congo was incapable and ineffective, or that it connived at cruelties and injustice and left them unredressed and unpunished, or that it deliberately perpetrated such cruelties and injustice itself or through its agents. That had been assumed all through the meeting. (Voices : "Proved.") The atrocities had also been assumed. (Cries of I" Nonsense " and" Proved.") They had been told of rumours and rumours of rumours (A Voice: "An eye-witness" and numerous interruptions.) The Rev. J. F. MaTTHEws : I move that Father Wilson has a respectful bearing. The Rev. HARVEY COOK: I second it. It is very bad behaviour not to give a man the right to reply. Father WILSON, continuing, said they had not had put before them such evidence as he believed any magistrate in the country would convict a lad of the pettiest of potty larcenies upon(Cries of "Nonsense.") There had not been a single case of atrocity given, in which the names I of the perpetrator and of the victim, the place or date I at which it occurred, had been given. Evidence with- out those primal elements was not worth considering. ' (" Rot.") On the contrary he bad abundant evidence I to offer in support of statements the very opposite of I those which the speakers had failed to prove and which I perhaps they did not even attempt to prove. There was I •the evidence of Stanley. The Rev. J. H. HA alas : Of twenty-three years ago. Father WILSON: Not all of it. There are also the statments of Sir Harry Johnson and many others. I have no interest except that of truth, charity and peace. (Applause.) lam not afraid of the ultimate outcome of the agitation which has heen called the shame of England by a person who bad a very good title to speak. I have full confidence in the democracy of this country that it will not permit a second time any such iniquitous blunder as took place at the Jameson raid --(applause and dissent)—which was followed by the worse blunder of the South African war. Our memories are not so short, but that we can still have in mind the sort of agitation that went on before those events. The CHAIRMAN: : I must ask you to keep to the subject. Father WILSON: I submit lan keeping to the sub- ject and I will end by saying I don't believe that either in your life-time or in mine the peopla of this country will risk the horrors of such a war as that or risk what may follow from England taking individual action, as she has no right to even under the article which has been quoted-36 I think it is—which provides that .ej I „the Sittflattsrc rsnwal.. _ Father WILSON': I have full trust that you will not risk such an awful loss in life and property. (Laughter.) You may laugh, but the agitation before the South African war was very much the same as this. ("Nonsense.") A VOICE : I wish to support the amendment. The CHAIRMAN: I cannot allow another speech. The Rev. J. EL HARRIS in reply said he was more sorry than he could express that the rev, father had said he (Mr. Harris) had given no evidence. (" Shame.") The most charitable construction he was prepared to ' put upon it was that their friend was not listening. He (Mr. Harris) was prepared to go before any magistrate they selected and swear upon oath, giving the names of people murdered and subjected to atrocities. Further he was prepared to swear before a magistrate that King Leopold's own Commission signed a statement he had provionsly read, that from evidence he brought before them it was proved that hundreds of people, including chiefs, men, women and little children, had been dons to death in his district alone for rubber, and that he could prove it by a multitude of witnesses. (Loud and prolonged cheers.) He was also prepared to swear to the genuineness of any photograph that bore the name of his wife or himself, and to give the names and the place of abode of every man whose photograph was shown. (Applause.) Stanley had not been to thh</p>
        <p>Congo for twenty-threo years, and a statement of Sir Harry Johnson's had already been read by the Chairman. He would be exceedingly sorry to think there was any possibility of war before the country, and as an Enelishman he would be more than sorry to think this Empire would ever annex one single inch of Congo territory. (Applause.) Ho sincerely hoped Belgium herself would see her way clear to annex the Congo under the terms of the Berlin Act. The Rev. Father WfurrLE, from the audience, asked _</p>
        <p>Councillor rOOLE : May I read one or two pars- Mr. Harris if the atrocities were still going on?' graphs of the last Consular report? Mr. HARRIS said he received news from a colleague The CHAIRMAN : I don't think so. I think before I on Mendel that the same atrocities had quite recently allow questions it is due to the meeting that the resolu- been repeated in that very district. Details were not tion should be proposed. yet to hand, but the people were being treated worse • than ever. He would, however, ask them not to empha- RESOLUPION OF PROTEST. also the atrocities so much but to think about the The M.tvott said he had no feeling in the matter system. except that theatrocitiesshould be stopped. (Applause.) Father WHITTLE : We are called here to protest He was forbidden by the position he occupied as chief against the atrocities. That is the chief thing. magistrate to take any part whatever in political con- Mr. HARRIS : I take it we are protesting againot the troveries—(applause from a section of the audience)— system which is the cause of the atrocities. but he felt this was neither a political nor a religious On a vote being taken the amendment was defeated question. It was a matter of brother to brother and and the resolution of protest was delared carried by an sister to sister. Therefore he had no hesitation in pro- overwhelming majority. posing: "That this meeting protests against the system The Rev. C. H. HARVEY-COOK moved a vote of thanks of spoliation and its inevitable barbarities introduced to the speakers and Chairman. into the Congo by the Sovereign of the Congo Free Captain CROSFIELD seconded. He said he had a State and relies upon the refusal of his Majesty's great admiration for Father Wilson but he must con- Government to sanction any scheme of administration fess he thought the rev. gentleman had on that occasion for the Congo territory which does not maintain intact got hold of the wrong end of the stick. (Applause.) the principles so explicitly laid down in the General Most of them could tell when a man was speaking the Act of Berlin for the maintenance of the rights and truth and he was sure there was such a ring of earnest- liberties of the native races." (Apprause.) ness and truth about Mr. Harris's statments, that it The RECTOR seconded. They bad seen from the was extraordinary how anyone could doubt them. photographs exhibited before the meeting what the (Applause.) Flaying quoted from a speech of Lord Congo was previous to the passing of the Act of Berlin Lansdowne's, Captain Crosfield said he had heard and what its condition was now, and they had heard enough to convince him that if other nations were not from Mr. Harris who had seen with his own eyes the going to act, we had got to act and he hoped we were barbarities that had taken place. They did not doubt Prepared to push the matter to a point, aye, to the his word. (Applause.) They had to discriminate point of the bayonet if necessary. (Applause.) between the King of Belgium and his Government on The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS supported and the Rev. the one hand and the people on the other, but he could Father WILSON added a testimony to the way in which not exeronerate the King and the Government from the Chairman had conducted the meeting. blame. His Majesty's promises seemed to have been The vote was heartily accorded and in responding most unreliable; and the motives for the atrocities Mr. HARRIS said he was prepared to say that if King were awfully mercenary. We were a civilised and a Leopold a ished to do so he could not stop what was powerful nation and men of honour and we bad pledged going on in the Congo Valley. 'l'he machinery was ourselves twenty years ago to see that the State was invented ; the system he had created had got beyond properly governed. We were also a Christian people the control of the Government and to attempt to and we were not, he hoped, going to stand any further modify it and to remodel the administration