      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>NO RESIGNATION.</head>
        <p>NO RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>DR. HAROLD JOSEPH RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD WESLEYANISM.</p>
        <p>Opening of the New Sunday School.</p>
        <p>Thursday was a day to be long remembered in the history of Latchford Wesleyan Methodism, for it witnessed the opening of the new Sunday school which i has been erected adjoining the Church n Knutsford- road. The premises have been carefully planned with a view to future extension without materially inter- fering with the present school accommodation, which consists of a well-lighted assembly hall, 60ft. by 301 t. having a raised platform at one end, an infants room, 26ft. by 14ft., and three classrooms, averaging 17ft. by 12f1. each, in one of which a tea-boiler aud washing-up accommodation is provided. At the end of the assembly hall a suitable room is provided for the storing ef crockery and tea urns. etc. The assembly hall ip con- nected with the chapel by means of a glazed sliding screen, which when open enables a portion of the hall to be utilized for extra sitting accommodation on festive occasions. The buildings throughout have been heated with hot water with small bore pipes and radiators on the low pressure principle and provision has been made for ample ventila- tion. The rooms are fitted with wood dadoes, the walls above being plastered and coloured and the ceiling of the assembly hall has in addition been ornamented with imitation spars stained and varnished to a suitable colour. Access to the assembly hall, with separate entrance to the infants room, is obtained by asphalted walks from the main entrance gates. The work has been carried out in a most satisfactory manner by Mr. Joseph Dolan, builder of Warrington from plans and instructions by Mr. S. P. Silcock, architect, of Warrington. There was a large attendance at the opening of the school by Mr. John Paylor, L.D.S. Amongst those present were the Revs. J. Gunnell, W. Mellor, T. Miller, E. J. Gawne and A. Freeman (of Harrogate, formerly of Warrington), Alderman Ellison, Messrs. J. W. Hayward, I. Johnson and S. P. Silcock (architect). The Rev. W. Mellor said he did not think Noncon formists had any too much ceremony, and therefore it was very proper that they should inaugurate that new school with a little ceremony. No one was more fitted to open the school than Mr. John Taylor, who was one of their trustees and had taken a deep interest in the work from the commencement. He believed all present would agree that they at Latchford had now one of the best schools in the circuit, if not the best. It was per- haps not the largest, but he thought it was the most convenient in many respects and theylhad reason to be- proud of it. (Applause.) In asking Mr. Taylor to open the school, Mr. Mellor said it was the intention of the congregation to present him with a large photo- graph of the premises framed in gold and fumed oak, which would be ready in a few days. Mr. Taylor then unlocked the door and declared the building open "in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Ghost." The company then entered the school and Mr. ' Meldrum (circuit steward) moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Taylor. He hoped other churches in the circuit would be stirred to emulate Latchford's example in providing such a fine school. ( Applause.) The Rev. J. Gunnell seconded and paid a high tribute to the work of the Latchford friends who, he said, had toiled hard and tong. That was pre-eminently a - working-class movement and the large subscriptions, he believed, were few. Mr. Wood (local trustee) supported and the vote was heartily agreed to. Mr. Taylor in responding said the day was a red letter day in the history of the young people of Latch- ford, but one could poitatielesb 31* Yttiehbinbntard of the " ea little ini‘tjt building was a memorial. Some three years ago there was a debt of £3OO on the place, but the people worked with such harmony and zeal that it was cleared off. (Applause.) The new school was certainly a great credit to both builder and architect— (hear, hear, and applause)—and Mr. Silcock was to be very warmly complimented upon the design. The meeting then concluded and a service was held in the church, the Rev. A. Freeman preaching. Afterwards tea was served. THE EVENING MEETING. In the evening a meeting was held in the new school, Mr. C. E. Parker presiding. The Rev. W. Miller presented the report on behalf of the treasurer, Mr. T. Strange, which stated that the church was built in 1891. Miss Hartley set the scheme for a new school going by giving the first donation of ' £loo.(Applause.) The cost of the building was estimated at £l,ooo.The forms which were in the school were made by members of the congregation. (Applause.) The timber was found for them and the members did the rest after they had done a hard day's work. They had saved at least thirty pounds in that way. The tea urns were also made by the members of their church and they had been presented with a gross of china. (Applause.) Mention must also be made of the ladies who had voluntarily cleaned the school out. The following was a list of the chief donations :—Miss Hartley, £130; Mrs. Hartley, £125; Hr. C. E. Parker, £3O; Mr. T.Strange, £l5 155.; Mr. William Bennett,2lo ; Mr. Heyward, £10; Sunday school, £37 7s. 7d.; Women's Bible Class, £lO ; Men's Bible Class, £5; Wesleyan school, £1; the Christmas choir collected £l3 11s. od. ; Sale of work, £5l 18s. 9d. The total amount sub- scribed to-date was £5lO is. 3d. (Applause.) The Rev. J. F. Matthews in emphasising the need for consistency in religious life said that if they wanted' to have a faithful Wesleyan Methodist he must be a Wesleyan Methodist from birth and die a Wesleyan Methodist and then they were sure that he was true to his religion. (Applause.) The Rev. Thomas Miller spoke of the work which their Sunday schools were doing. He thought they were- spending too much money on treats. Then again, he had visited a Sunday school and had found the teacher reading from a novel instead of reading from a Bible. They wanted Sunday school teachers who taught the true religion and believed it and not those who taught what they did not believe in. (Applause.) He concluded by saying that he hoped they would have a very successful evening and that they would have the whole of the debt cleared off the school in a very short period. (Applause.) The Rev. Alfred Freeman delivered an encouraging address. Mr. C. E. Parker said he hoped the relations between all the churches would continue to be friendly. He pointed out the value of the increased accommodation in their Sund iy school work and trusted their labours would be abundantly successful. (Applause.) The treasurer, Mr. T. Strange, announced that the collection had amounted to £2 10s. 7d. and that he had received another £lO which made the grand and substan- tial total of £552 us. 10d. (Applause.) A vote of thanks was iucorded to the Rev. A. Freeman for his visit and to the Chairman for presiding.-</p>
        <p>Wr.rrington.—Printed and published by JOHN WALKEN &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printing Works- Bewsey-street, and published by them at the same place, and also at The Examiner Offices, Victoria-road WIDNES: 32, George-street, ST. lIELENS: 3, Market.. treat, EARLESTOWN; Church-street BUNCO= Nov 30th. 1907.</p>
